1
00:02:13,205 --> 00:02:15,873
We should say goodbye to her
before she goes.

2
00:02:16,142 --> 00:02:17,008
Well done.

3
00:02:35,394 --> 00:02:38,896
(Hippo)

4
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:02,721
Why is everyone so quiet?

5
00:03:03,623 --> 00:03:06,659
You guys were all about meetings
and acting so smart earlier.

6
00:03:13,899 --> 00:03:17,535
I'd like to say something
as one of the voters.

7
00:03:20,506 --> 00:03:22,474
You were elected by our votes,

8
00:03:22,475 --> 00:03:23,842
and since it was your call,

9
00:03:23,843 --> 00:03:26,845
we have no choice but to accept it.

10
00:03:27,046 --> 00:03:29,348
I get that, but I just have one question.

11
00:03:29,655 --> 00:03:32,090
You were in that meeting
for over ten minutes,

12
00:03:32,517 --> 00:03:34,452
almost right up to the deadline.

13
00:03:34,687 --> 00:03:36,755
What took you so long?

14
00:03:40,926 --> 00:03:44,328
The main thing we were debating was...

15
00:03:44,964 --> 00:03:48,667
whether we'd agreed to use
the immunity card in the first place.

16
00:03:48,668 --> 00:03:51,903
Did you save Michael,
or did you eliminate Hippo?

17
00:03:54,307 --> 00:03:55,207
In that meeting.

18
00:03:56,175 --> 00:03:59,878
If you chose to eliminate Hippo,
what was the reason?

19
00:04:02,615 --> 00:04:05,050
First off, when it came to saving him,

20
00:04:06,218 --> 00:04:08,352
I think we can all agree...

21
00:04:08,353 --> 00:04:10,556
that he was the first
to raise suspicions about a spy,

22
00:04:10,557 --> 00:04:13,858
and he was the most passionate
about finding out.

23
00:04:13,859 --> 00:04:16,494
When we were pairing up 1-on-1,

24
00:04:16,495 --> 00:04:18,397
there were times he hesitated...

25
00:04:18,422 --> 00:04:21,799
because he was worried
about being targeted.

26
00:04:21,800 --> 00:04:24,869
I told him that if he got eliminated,
I'd use my immunity card on him,

27
00:04:24,870 --> 00:04:28,072
so he should go ahead...

28
00:04:28,274 --> 00:04:30,308
and help keep our community safe.

29
00:04:30,409 --> 00:04:32,877
So my personal promise to him...

30
00:04:32,878 --> 00:04:36,547
ended up clashing
with what I promised everyone...

31
00:04:36,916 --> 00:04:39,184
during my campaign.

32
00:04:39,185 --> 00:04:41,686
It wasn't something I fully aligned
with the other leaders.

33
00:04:41,687 --> 00:04:43,054
I just felt...

34
00:04:43,489 --> 00:04:46,057
bound to keep my word.

35
00:04:46,058 --> 00:04:48,460
You've been the most vocal...

36
00:04:49,037 --> 00:04:53,131
about building an ideal society
and looking out for the vulnerable.

37
00:04:53,766 --> 00:04:56,368
But you just said you saved him...

38
00:04:56,369 --> 00:04:58,303
because of a personal promise you made...

39
00:04:59,038 --> 00:05:02,274
back when you were running.

40
00:05:04,076 --> 00:05:08,012
Eleven of us voted for you today
to protect the vulnerable,

41
00:05:08,507 --> 00:05:11,242
and what you just did completely
contradicts that.

42
00:05:15,154 --> 00:05:17,689
If you compare Michael and Hippo,

43
00:05:17,690 --> 00:05:21,359
by the numbers alone,
she seemed far more vulnerable.

44
00:05:22,827 --> 00:05:23,992
We voted for you today...

45
00:05:24,017 --> 00:05:26,030
because you promised to stand up
for people like her.

46
00:05:26,031 --> 00:05:28,699
I think we might just define...

47
00:05:29,168 --> 00:05:31,802
who is actually vulnerable differently.

48
00:05:33,539 --> 00:05:35,707
By the numbers alone,
she might seem that way.

49
00:05:36,242 --> 00:05:38,076
But looking at what he put on the line,

50
00:05:38,077 --> 00:05:40,946
some might see him
as the more vulnerable one.

51
00:05:40,947 --> 00:05:42,914
- That's just my perspective.
- I'm sorry,

52
00:05:42,915 --> 00:05:45,317
but I have to push back on that.

53
00:05:45,985 --> 00:05:47,786
The reason
I brought up vulnerability is...

54
00:05:47,787 --> 00:05:50,022
if you had simply said
he felt more vulnerable to you,

55
00:05:50,023 --> 00:05:51,356
I probably would've let it go.

56
00:05:51,690 --> 00:05:54,859
But you said you were torn
over a promise you made him.

57
00:05:54,860 --> 00:05:56,594
That pits your personal promise...

58
00:05:56,595 --> 00:05:59,897
directly against the commitment
you made to all of us...

59
00:05:59,898 --> 00:06:01,832
when you ran for leader.

60
00:06:04,069 --> 00:06:06,570
I know I can't completely
justify my decision.

61
00:06:06,571 --> 00:06:07,905
To be completely honest,

62
00:06:08,007 --> 00:06:09,708
we didn't have hours...

63
00:06:09,709 --> 00:06:11,509
to sit around and talk it through.

64
00:06:11,744 --> 00:06:14,979
There just wasn't enough time
to reach a rock-solid,

65
00:06:14,980 --> 00:06:17,515
perfectly reasoned consensus...

66
00:06:17,850 --> 00:06:20,084
that would satisfy everyone.

67
00:06:20,085 --> 00:06:22,487
Put simply...

68
00:06:22,488 --> 00:06:23,721
Sorry to cut in,

69
00:06:26,459 --> 00:06:30,896
but I really just want to ask
one simple thing.

70
00:06:31,163 --> 00:06:32,964
Did the four of you genuinely believe...

71
00:06:33,833 --> 00:06:35,233
that was the best call?

72
00:06:42,107 --> 00:06:44,776
People voted for her, thinking,

73
00:06:44,777 --> 00:06:48,512
"If I'm on the chopping block,"

74
00:06:48,513 --> 00:06:50,414
"I'd want her to use that card on me."

75
00:06:50,892 --> 00:06:53,260
But today,
all these smart folks froze up...

76
00:06:53,285 --> 00:06:54,920
and couldn't give a straight answer.

77
00:06:55,387 --> 00:06:58,556
The whole thing just looked bad.

78
00:07:04,129 --> 00:07:06,597
The four of them
made this decision together,

79
00:07:06,598 --> 00:07:08,167
so let's stop putting her on the spot.

80
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:10,735
Leadership carries heavy responsibility.

81
00:07:10,736 --> 00:07:13,704
She was thrown into a tough spot,
and things got confusing.

82
00:07:13,705 --> 00:07:15,673
We've made our point,

83
00:07:15,674 --> 00:07:17,775
so let's just move forward.

84
00:07:17,776 --> 00:07:20,411
I wasn't asking out of emotion.

85
00:07:20,436 --> 00:07:21,436
Fair enough.

86
00:07:21,747 --> 00:07:23,848
It must've been hard on everyone.

87
00:07:24,216 --> 00:07:27,785
I don't think anyone doubts
how difficult that call was.

88
00:07:27,920 --> 00:07:31,055
Given our daily schedule,

89
00:07:31,357 --> 00:07:33,291
there really isn't enough time...

90
00:07:33,292 --> 00:07:36,996
to talk through every detail
and plan for every scenario.

91
00:07:37,496 --> 00:07:41,299
It isn't fair to put all the blame
on the leader.

92
00:07:41,800 --> 00:07:44,101
In that position,

93
00:07:44,102 --> 00:07:46,171
who can honestly say...

94
00:07:46,172 --> 00:07:48,272
they would've handled it any better?

95
00:07:48,641 --> 00:07:50,841
This was our first elimination,

96
00:07:52,077 --> 00:07:54,645
- and nobody feels good about it.
- Exactly.

97
00:07:54,646 --> 00:07:56,747
I know my decision wasn't perfect.

98
00:07:56,748 --> 00:07:59,350
I'm sure some of you feel...

99
00:07:59,351 --> 00:08:01,719
it went against my platform,

100
00:08:01,954 --> 00:08:04,923
or that it was simply a bad call.

101
00:08:04,924 --> 00:08:07,458
Plenty of people might see it that way.

102
00:08:08,670 --> 00:08:12,363
But I'll learn from this
and take your feedback to heart.

103
00:08:12,364 --> 00:08:14,599
I'll do better next time.

104
00:08:14,600 --> 00:08:15,867
I'm truly sorry.

105
00:08:17,670 --> 00:08:18,737
I felt completely alone.

106
00:08:19,038 --> 00:08:20,471
Being a leader...

107
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:22,073
is lonely.

108
00:08:22,485 --> 00:08:26,487
The entire burden falls on you.

109
00:08:26,512 --> 00:08:28,180
(Polar Bear: left, feminism,
working-class, open-minded)

110
00:08:28,181 --> 00:08:29,181
And it's so hard...

111
00:08:30,248 --> 00:08:33,116
to open up to anyone
about what's weighing on you.

112
00:08:33,420 --> 00:08:34,728
It's only going to get tougher.

113
00:08:34,753 --> 00:08:36,287
Every time I make a choice,

114
00:08:36,789 --> 00:08:38,722
someone will end up disappointed,

115
00:08:38,992 --> 00:08:41,092
and someone will end up hurt.

116
00:08:43,195 --> 00:08:44,430
That's just how it is.

117
00:08:45,831 --> 00:08:47,065
(The announcement will now begin.)

118
00:08:53,172 --> 00:08:55,974
With the ideology check complete,

119
00:08:56,875 --> 00:09:01,045
we will now reveal everyone's code.

120
00:09:03,015 --> 00:09:07,652
Resident Go Ae Shin's code.

121
00:09:12,658 --> 00:09:14,826
Why are they exposing this?
It's uncomfortable.

122
00:09:15,828 --> 00:09:19,164
- Write it down.
- Politics, left, one point.

123
00:09:19,965 --> 00:09:22,767
Gender, feminism, one point.

124
00:09:22,768 --> 00:09:24,258
When those numbers flashed on the screen,

125
00:09:24,283 --> 00:09:26,637
I thought, "What's the point? It's over."

126
00:09:26,940 --> 00:09:28,073
But before I knew it,

127
00:09:28,074 --> 00:09:29,875
I was writing them down anyway,
just in case.

128
00:09:30,533 --> 00:09:33,445
- Class, working-class, one point.
- You and I have the exact same code.

129
00:09:34,113 --> 00:09:36,202
Michael.

130
00:09:36,226 --> 00:09:37,882
Superman.

131
00:09:37,883 --> 00:09:40,051
- It's all blue.
- The code is...

132
00:09:40,319 --> 00:09:42,020
You know what's funny?

133
00:09:42,121 --> 00:09:44,622
Superman and I clashed constantly,

134
00:09:44,623 --> 00:09:46,858
yet we have the exact same color.

135
00:09:47,092 --> 00:09:50,094
Polar Bear was my complete opposite,

136
00:09:50,095 --> 00:09:52,430
yet she saved me, and I backed her.

137
00:09:53,065 --> 00:09:57,502
Resident Hippo's code.

138
00:09:58,103 --> 00:10:00,172
- Called it.
- Politics, left, three points.

139
00:10:00,173 --> 00:10:01,439
I knew it.

140
00:10:01,841 --> 00:10:04,542
Gender, feminism, three points.

141
00:10:06,211 --> 00:10:10,447
Resident Sugar's code.

142
00:10:11,450 --> 00:10:13,518
Class, upper-middle, two points.

143
00:10:14,319 --> 00:10:17,489
- Born with a silver spoon.
- Openness, open-minded, one point.

144
00:10:20,159 --> 00:10:21,326
Figures.

145
00:10:21,693 --> 00:10:23,662
So many of them are upper-middle class.

146
00:10:24,029 --> 00:10:25,363
All of them are Us.

147
00:10:25,631 --> 00:10:29,033
Must be nice starting life on easy mode.

148
00:10:29,268 --> 00:10:31,436
Real life isn't like that.

149
00:10:31,704 --> 00:10:33,071
In other words,

150
00:10:33,072 --> 00:10:36,107
they didn't speak from lived experience.

151
00:10:36,108 --> 00:10:39,812
It's just book knowledge. Pure theory.

152
00:10:39,813 --> 00:10:41,446
They're just smooth talkers.

153
00:10:41,513 --> 00:10:44,458
Studying something doesn't mean
you actually know how it works.

154
00:10:44,483 --> 00:10:46,617
You have to get your hands dirty, sweat,

155
00:10:46,618 --> 00:10:50,621
and experience it firsthand.
That's real learning.

156
00:10:50,723 --> 00:10:54,092
People with real-world experience
should be leading the way.

157
00:10:55,561 --> 00:10:57,762
You may now disclose...

158
00:10:57,763 --> 00:11:01,132
your age and occupation.

159
00:11:01,133 --> 00:11:03,201
(Age and occupation may now be revealed.)

160
00:11:03,302 --> 00:11:06,537
Today's favorability vote
will now commence.

161
00:11:06,538 --> 00:11:07,672
The favorability vote, now?

162
00:11:07,673 --> 00:11:09,807
- Right after that?
- Please proceed to...

163
00:11:09,808 --> 00:11:10,842
the Community Center...

164
00:11:11,076 --> 00:11:13,811
- and cast your votes.
- This place is ruthless.

165
00:11:13,812 --> 00:11:14,880
It is what it is.

166
00:11:14,980 --> 00:11:16,213
Let's go vote.

167
00:11:16,214 --> 00:11:18,249
(Favorability Vote)

168
00:11:21,153 --> 00:11:23,888
What are cumulative
favorability scores used for?

169
00:11:24,022 --> 00:11:27,891
They may later serve as criteria
for resident eliminations.

170
00:11:31,797 --> 00:11:34,565
(Gray, Nangja)

171
00:11:36,869 --> 00:11:38,002
(Dark Knight)

172
00:11:38,003 --> 00:11:40,606
- That shows their true colors, right?
- Sure does.

173
00:11:41,707 --> 00:11:43,842
Those guys were acting
all high and mighty.

174
00:11:43,843 --> 00:11:45,110
All talk, no action.

175
00:11:45,711 --> 00:11:46,944
- Totally.
- See?

176
00:11:46,945 --> 00:11:49,448
It would've been much neater
to just use the pass right from the start.

177
00:11:49,982 --> 00:11:51,950
They made sure to keep
an extra life for themselves.

178
00:11:52,484 --> 00:11:54,652
Basically bought themselves a safety net.

179
00:11:54,653 --> 00:11:55,519
Exactly.

180
00:11:55,520 --> 00:11:58,088
Why bother making excuses?

181
00:11:58,089 --> 00:12:00,926
We all know they wouldn't have used it,
no matter what.

182
00:12:00,927 --> 00:12:03,561
- The truth is out.
- Why would they give it up?

183
00:12:03,562 --> 00:12:04,997
Never in a million years.

184
00:12:06,165 --> 00:12:07,432
- Just rationalizing.
- Absolutely.

185
00:12:07,433 --> 00:12:08,834
When push came to shove.

186
00:12:09,468 --> 00:12:11,936
Are they that desperate to survive?

187
00:12:12,437 --> 00:12:13,437
Seriously.

188
00:12:14,673 --> 00:12:16,708
Holding an immunity card
comes with massive pressure.

189
00:12:17,609 --> 00:12:18,842
Huge pressure.

190
00:12:20,612 --> 00:12:22,446
A better decision...

191
00:12:23,082 --> 00:12:25,816
Would we have caught this much flak...

192
00:12:25,817 --> 00:12:28,352
if we had chosen Hippo instead?

193
00:12:28,554 --> 00:12:30,489
- Would've been the exact same.
- Yeah, no difference.

194
00:12:39,097 --> 00:12:42,232
(Dark Knight, Michael)

195
00:12:44,903 --> 00:12:46,036
(Dark Knight)

196
00:12:46,738 --> 00:12:47,538
(Dark Knight)

197
00:12:47,539 --> 00:12:50,607
(Dark Knight is sweeping the votes.)

198
00:12:54,813 --> 00:12:56,514
(Superman)

199
00:12:56,515 --> 00:12:58,115
(Polar Bear)

200
00:13:02,521 --> 00:13:05,590
(Ted was disappointed
by Polar Bear's broken promise.)

201
00:13:05,682 --> 00:13:08,116
What you just did goes
completely against that.

202
00:13:08,594 --> 00:13:10,328
We spent days building up that momentum,

203
00:13:10,353 --> 00:13:12,264
and it all just crumbled.

204
00:13:12,731 --> 00:13:14,765
Her choice disappointed me.

205
00:13:15,701 --> 00:13:16,968
I called her out on it...

206
00:13:16,969 --> 00:13:19,037
and made things awkward.

207
00:13:19,638 --> 00:13:20,838
But I still voted for her...

208
00:13:20,839 --> 00:13:22,641
because I wanted
to clear the air and move on.

209
00:13:33,711 --> 00:13:36,713
- Vote for each other?
- You bet.

210
00:13:40,692 --> 00:13:41,892
(Go Ae Shin)

211
00:13:43,562 --> 00:13:45,963
(Polar Bear, Benjamin)

212
00:13:46,224 --> 00:13:50,260
(Genie voted for the immunity holders.)

213
00:13:50,669 --> 00:13:53,337
The favorability vote has concluded.

214
00:13:54,686 --> 00:13:59,089
Furthermore, today's evictee, Hippo,

215
00:13:59,378 --> 00:14:00,878
- Here it comes.
- was...

216
00:14:02,748 --> 00:14:03,715
not the spy.

217
00:14:05,217 --> 00:14:06,083
Knew it.

218
00:14:15,627 --> 00:14:18,529
All official activities for today
have concluded.

219
00:14:19,031 --> 00:14:21,432
The Community Center is now closed.

220
00:14:25,504 --> 00:14:26,870
We wish you a peaceful night.

221
00:14:33,278 --> 00:14:34,411
(Tension Under One Roof)

222
00:14:34,713 --> 00:14:36,914
(The Community)

223
00:14:36,915 --> 00:14:41,419
(9. Who Is Guilty?)

224
00:14:41,486 --> 00:14:42,553
(Day 7, morning)

225
00:14:42,554 --> 00:14:44,021
I was lying in bed...

226
00:14:45,057 --> 00:14:47,859
After our talk last night,

227
00:14:50,362 --> 00:14:52,597
all those lingering suspicions
came flooding back.

228
00:14:53,717 --> 00:14:55,167
The pieces started falling into place.

229
00:14:56,401 --> 00:14:59,903
I had this sudden "aha" moment.

230
00:15:01,506 --> 00:15:04,241
There's still one puzzle
I can't crack, though.

231
00:15:04,242 --> 00:15:05,142
Benjamin.

232
00:15:05,311 --> 00:15:06,510
His entire profile...

233
00:15:06,645 --> 00:15:08,112
is fabricated.

234
00:15:08,513 --> 00:15:09,446
He's an attorney.

235
00:15:10,248 --> 00:15:11,817
But he hasn't mentioned it once.

236
00:15:12,884 --> 00:15:15,419
Everyone is dying to figure you out.

237
00:15:15,420 --> 00:15:17,923
But you always keep your cards
close to your chest.

238
00:15:21,460 --> 00:15:23,327
You may now disclose...

239
00:15:23,328 --> 00:15:26,364
your age and occupation.

240
00:15:26,365 --> 00:15:27,098
(Last night)

241
00:15:27,099 --> 00:15:28,666
Let's go around and introduce ourselves.

242
00:15:28,667 --> 00:15:30,401
The People Power Party's
local committee chair for Dobong-gap.

243
00:15:33,191 --> 00:15:35,458
I served
as the youngest presidential secretary...

244
00:15:35,483 --> 00:15:36,672
under the Moon Jae In administration.

245
00:15:37,442 --> 00:15:38,443
That's awesome.

246
00:15:38,877 --> 00:15:40,477
I started a company...

247
00:15:40,478 --> 00:15:43,647
and was COO until last year.

248
00:15:43,996 --> 00:15:47,018
The company worked
on a lot of different projects.

249
00:15:47,319 --> 00:15:48,352
He's a lawyer.

250
00:15:49,087 --> 00:15:51,422
We developed an online platform...

251
00:15:51,423 --> 00:15:53,924
and an AI-powered...

252
00:15:54,359 --> 00:15:55,893
It's all made up.

253
00:15:56,806 --> 00:15:59,472
HR system.

254
00:15:59,497 --> 00:16:01,333
We had our hands in a variety of things.

255
00:16:02,934 --> 00:16:05,870
So he's the CEO of a company.

256
00:16:05,871 --> 00:16:07,572
Isn't he the one making
over 300,000 dollars a year?

257
00:16:07,906 --> 00:16:08,873
Could be.

258
00:16:15,681 --> 00:16:18,182
I need to tell you something.

259
00:16:19,084 --> 00:16:21,485
You know how they revealed
everyone's code...

260
00:16:22,454 --> 00:16:23,721
yesterday.

261
00:16:24,289 --> 00:16:27,091
If you really had to go after someone,

262
00:16:27,393 --> 00:16:30,416
Benjamin is an easy mark
since he only has four points.

263
00:16:30,796 --> 00:16:32,297
Based on the things he's said,

264
00:16:32,298 --> 00:16:35,199
I had a pretty good guess about his code.

265
00:16:35,333 --> 00:16:38,101
When I was getting ready to target him,

266
00:16:38,737 --> 00:16:42,240
I replayed everything he'd said
in my head,

267
00:16:42,674 --> 00:16:44,708
- and something just didn't add up.
- Didn't add up?

268
00:16:45,410 --> 00:16:46,377
First of all,

269
00:16:46,378 --> 00:16:49,480
nobody in the chat seems
to struggle with Korean at all.

270
00:16:49,481 --> 00:16:51,148
That's so true.

271
00:16:51,984 --> 00:16:53,984
If anyone struggles with the chat,
it's me.

272
00:16:53,985 --> 00:16:54,918
I know.

273
00:16:55,287 --> 00:16:56,820
So it's weird.

274
00:16:57,622 --> 00:16:59,523
Like I said,

275
00:16:59,524 --> 00:17:00,991
I was a bit suspicious of Ted.

276
00:17:00,992 --> 00:17:02,726
He was the most trusted person here.

277
00:17:02,727 --> 00:17:05,329
Nobody actually suspected Benjamin at all.

278
00:17:05,330 --> 00:17:07,764
I'm a bit curious, though.

279
00:17:08,133 --> 00:17:09,868
If he's that fluent in English,

280
00:17:10,035 --> 00:17:12,637
is it really that easy for him
to read those books?

281
00:17:12,638 --> 00:17:14,849
He's breezing through books
like "Game Theory,"

282
00:17:14,874 --> 00:17:16,073
and those aren't easy reads.

283
00:17:16,074 --> 00:17:17,428
Can he just pick those up and read them?

284
00:17:17,453 --> 00:17:20,277
Well, I guess he could manage.

285
00:17:20,834 --> 00:17:22,346
Still, something feels off.

286
00:17:22,581 --> 00:17:25,650
Then I'll keep an eye on him too.

287
00:17:25,750 --> 00:17:28,151
- Let's keep him on our radar.
- Let's watch him.

288
00:17:28,152 --> 00:17:28,952
Got it.

289
00:17:29,421 --> 00:17:31,289
I suspect he might be karen3000.

290
00:17:31,290 --> 00:17:34,258
Now that would be a twist.

291
00:17:37,996 --> 00:17:39,564
I wonder what we're doing today.

292
00:17:44,970 --> 00:17:47,339
Feels like we wake up in order of age.

293
00:17:47,739 --> 00:17:48,839
Good morning.

294
00:17:49,040 --> 00:17:49,940
Good morning.

295
00:17:50,775 --> 00:17:52,185
Why aren't the babies down yet?

296
00:17:52,210 --> 00:17:53,243
The babies.

297
00:17:55,080 --> 00:17:56,714
- You got a high score.
- What?

298
00:17:56,715 --> 00:17:58,784
- Your score is high.
- Honestly, I was surprised...

299
00:17:58,785 --> 00:18:00,718
to see my score that high.

300
00:18:01,346 --> 00:18:03,847
I used to be more in the center,

301
00:18:04,055 --> 00:18:08,492
but my views started shifting a bit
once I entered the workforce.

302
00:18:08,493 --> 00:18:10,227
Banu's score...

303
00:18:11,030 --> 00:18:12,896
was four.

304
00:18:13,832 --> 00:18:15,933
Her code was easy to call.

305
00:18:15,958 --> 00:18:18,827
- Really?
- Because she's from the Middle East.

306
00:18:18,872 --> 00:18:20,638
So she's bound to be open-minded.

307
00:18:20,815 --> 00:18:23,050
I was sure she'd lean right on openness.

308
00:18:23,075 --> 00:18:23,974
Does she lean right?

309
00:18:23,975 --> 00:18:25,142
- Yeah.
- Right.

310
00:18:25,143 --> 00:18:26,343
- Is that so?
- She's conservative.

311
00:18:26,344 --> 00:18:27,444
She's Muslim.

312
00:18:27,855 --> 00:18:30,857
I wouldn't have been surprised
to see a three from her on that.

313
00:18:30,882 --> 00:18:32,583
- Really?
- Very conservative.

314
00:18:32,584 --> 00:18:34,085
Gray, you're pretty far left, aren't you?

315
00:18:34,305 --> 00:18:36,988
No, I only got two points there,
not three.

316
00:18:37,303 --> 00:18:38,799
The only area where I lean right is class.

317
00:18:38,824 --> 00:18:39,990
Otherwise, I lean left across the board.

318
00:18:39,991 --> 00:18:40,992
You grew up wealthy?

319
00:18:41,760 --> 00:18:42,860
I suppose so.

320
00:18:43,261 --> 00:18:44,962
I could vote for someone
across the aisle...

321
00:18:44,963 --> 00:18:47,698
if I felt their policies were
genuinely better.

322
00:18:48,200 --> 00:18:49,300
I'd vote for them.

323
00:18:49,468 --> 00:18:51,402
Average folks like me...

324
00:18:51,836 --> 00:18:53,837
don't just pick a side
and stick to it blindly.

325
00:18:53,838 --> 00:18:56,173
You look at key policies,

326
00:18:56,174 --> 00:18:58,775
look at the candidate as a person,
and make up your mind.

327
00:18:58,776 --> 00:19:01,144
I'd never taken a test like that before,

328
00:19:01,145 --> 00:19:02,313
so I was surprised...

329
00:19:03,014 --> 00:19:04,916
my score came out higher than expected.

330
00:19:07,052 --> 00:19:09,320
- Morning.
- Good morning.

331
00:19:09,788 --> 00:19:11,422
- Morning.
- Good morning.

332
00:19:11,423 --> 00:19:12,624
(The residents start gathering
in the main hall.)

333
00:19:12,649 --> 00:19:14,267
You look like royalty today.

334
00:19:14,292 --> 00:19:15,125
This?

335
00:19:18,563 --> 00:19:19,429
Gray.

336
00:19:19,430 --> 00:19:22,432
Who did you back
in the favorability vote last night?

337
00:19:22,433 --> 00:19:23,367
I voted for you.

338
00:19:23,935 --> 00:19:26,271
So Superman didn't vote for me, then?

339
00:19:26,504 --> 00:19:28,004
- I'll have to ask him later.
- Okay.

340
00:19:28,306 --> 00:19:30,108
Did you guys swap votes last night?

341
00:19:30,575 --> 00:19:32,309
I think I voted for you.

342
00:19:32,310 --> 00:19:33,611
I didn't trade votes with anyone.

343
00:19:33,778 --> 00:19:35,145
I voted for you too.

344
00:19:36,514 --> 00:19:38,048
Nangja, last night,

345
00:19:38,483 --> 00:19:41,285
you voted for me, right?

346
00:19:41,286 --> 00:19:42,152
Yeah.

347
00:19:42,153 --> 00:19:43,120
Hold on.

348
00:19:43,421 --> 00:19:46,289
I'm down one heart.

349
00:19:46,290 --> 00:19:47,257
He...

350
00:19:48,026 --> 00:19:49,760
seems desperate...

351
00:19:50,528 --> 00:19:51,761
to survive.

352
00:19:53,064 --> 00:19:54,699
- He's sitting on a lot of cash.
- Yeah.

353
00:19:55,200 --> 00:19:56,634
I bet it's Gray.

354
00:19:57,402 --> 00:19:58,202
Same here.

355
00:19:58,703 --> 00:20:01,605
Hippo and Michael were the ones
who started getting suspicious...

356
00:20:01,606 --> 00:20:02,806
of him yesterday.

357
00:20:02,807 --> 00:20:04,841
Here's my take.

358
00:20:05,110 --> 00:20:07,645
With Hippo, Gray was...

359
00:20:10,081 --> 00:20:11,782
I think he took a shot at her code...

360
00:20:11,783 --> 00:20:13,784
and ended up taking her out.

361
00:20:13,785 --> 00:20:16,386
Michael and Gray are tight.

362
00:20:16,387 --> 00:20:19,022
Michael was sitting right next to me.

363
00:20:19,023 --> 00:20:22,060
He mentioned that Gray told him
he was going to eliminate Hippo.

364
00:20:23,028 --> 00:20:24,562
Keep this between us.

365
00:20:25,263 --> 00:20:27,164
You know Gray went after Hippo.

366
00:20:27,732 --> 00:20:29,199
I'm 100 percent sure.

367
00:20:29,834 --> 00:20:32,969
He missed on his first try,

368
00:20:32,970 --> 00:20:34,506
then nailed it on Round Three.

369
00:20:34,673 --> 00:20:36,508
I don't know who took out Michael, though.

370
00:20:37,042 --> 00:20:38,442
Could Gray have done that?

371
00:20:39,678 --> 00:20:41,212
Why would he do that?

372
00:20:42,614 --> 00:20:43,948
He definitely targeted Hippo.

373
00:20:45,450 --> 00:20:46,917
It doesn't make sense.

374
00:20:47,887 --> 00:20:48,752
I need some sugar.

375
00:20:48,820 --> 00:20:51,288
I really have my eye on Gray, though.

376
00:20:51,723 --> 00:20:53,491
- Really?
- Because yesterday,

377
00:20:53,992 --> 00:20:56,026
the two people pressing Gray
were Michael...

378
00:20:57,495 --> 00:20:58,361
and Hippo.

379
00:20:59,564 --> 00:21:03,334
So I went back and thought
about the things Gray kept saying.

380
00:21:03,335 --> 00:21:06,770
"People might tell a little lie early on
to protect their allies."

381
00:21:06,971 --> 00:21:08,405
He kept hammering that point.

382
00:21:08,406 --> 00:21:09,873
He wouldn't stop bringing it up.

383
00:21:09,874 --> 00:21:11,641
He kept saying,
"Someone could totally do that."

384
00:21:11,843 --> 00:21:14,378
Which basically means he's lied before.

385
00:21:14,379 --> 00:21:15,946
Right. Exactly.

386
00:21:15,947 --> 00:21:17,415
- We're on the same page.
- Definitely.

387
00:21:17,416 --> 00:21:19,650
- Picking up on the same vibe.
- Totally.

388
00:21:20,823 --> 00:21:22,720
What was that about?
Let's keep tabs on him.

389
00:21:31,262 --> 00:21:33,296
(The spy can check...)

390
00:21:33,297 --> 00:21:38,368
(everyone's personal funds every morning.)

391
00:21:39,671 --> 00:21:40,871
(Gray: 13,040 dollars)

392
00:21:40,872 --> 00:21:42,640
(Gray: 1st place
after taking Hippo's money)

393
00:21:42,641 --> 00:21:43,874
(Benjamin: 10,620 dollars,
2nd place after taking Michael's money)

394
00:21:43,875 --> 00:21:46,610
(Superman: 7,756.56 dollars, 3rd place)

395
00:21:46,611 --> 00:21:48,212
It was Gray.

396
00:21:48,213 --> 00:21:49,346
He eliminated Hippo.

397
00:21:49,347 --> 00:21:50,548
They're going to catch on.

398
00:21:50,549 --> 00:21:54,318
The more I talked to people,
the more nervous I got.

399
00:21:54,319 --> 00:21:56,620
The lies were starting to pile up.

400
00:21:56,788 --> 00:21:58,589
Benjamin's backstory is starting to crack.

401
00:21:58,824 --> 00:22:01,124
I only planned on keeping this persona
up for three days.

402
00:22:01,125 --> 00:22:03,794
I'm running out of material here.

403
00:22:04,163 --> 00:22:06,930
Gray broke the rule.

404
00:22:07,132 --> 00:22:11,035
Looks like we've got another villain
who's doing me a favor.

405
00:22:12,504 --> 00:22:13,571
Gray.

406
00:22:17,442 --> 00:22:18,810
Why did he eliminate her, though?

407
00:22:20,678 --> 00:22:24,648
(He found a scapegoat
to deflect the suspicion.)

408
00:22:37,762 --> 00:22:39,730
(The announcement will now begin.)

409
00:22:42,968 --> 00:22:45,835
This is an announcement
from the Community Center.

410
00:22:49,541 --> 00:22:51,775
Today, up to three residents...

411
00:22:52,076 --> 00:22:56,213
may be evicted...

412
00:22:56,214 --> 00:22:57,915
from the Community House.

413
00:22:57,916 --> 00:22:58,949
Up to three?

414
00:22:59,084 --> 00:22:59,983
That's huge.

415
00:22:59,984 --> 00:23:02,452
It said "may be evicted,"
so maybe nobody will leave.

416
00:23:02,453 --> 00:23:03,720
"May be evicted."

417
00:23:03,855 --> 00:23:06,090
Effective immediately,

418
00:23:06,091 --> 00:23:08,792
all entry and exit to the Community House
is restricted.

419
00:23:09,761 --> 00:23:11,528
Something is definitely up.

420
00:23:11,529 --> 00:23:13,931
- Just like when Banu arrived.
- Something is brewing for sure.

421
00:23:13,932 --> 00:23:15,699
Looks like more people are showing up.

422
00:23:21,539 --> 00:23:23,640
- What's that?
- What?

423
00:23:25,210 --> 00:23:26,010
What is going on?

424
00:23:45,430 --> 00:23:48,732
A crowd of strangers walked in
holding black notebooks.

425
00:23:48,767 --> 00:23:50,734
I didn't know what to make of it.

426
00:23:52,136 --> 00:23:53,770
One of them might recognize me.

427
00:23:53,771 --> 00:23:55,338
One slip of the tongue,

428
00:23:55,339 --> 00:23:57,273
and my whole cover is blown.

429
00:23:58,377 --> 00:24:00,545
- How many of them are there?
- That's a ton of people.

430
00:24:01,145 --> 00:24:03,213
- Easily over 30.
- Seriously.

431
00:24:03,257 --> 00:24:04,937
Maybe we have to win over people...

432
00:24:04,961 --> 00:24:06,693
whose beliefs are
the polar opposite of ours.

433
00:24:06,718 --> 00:24:10,020
If that's the drill,
they have the upper hand.

434
00:24:10,021 --> 00:24:14,157
A whole crowd marched right past us.

435
00:24:14,158 --> 00:24:16,561
I figured the challenge was probably
to win them over.

436
00:24:18,963 --> 00:24:21,665
What could this be? I'm stressing out.

437
00:24:24,636 --> 00:24:28,005
We will now begin
today's daytime schedule.

438
00:24:28,006 --> 00:24:28,873
Right off the bat?

439
00:24:29,374 --> 00:24:31,141
Today's daytime schedule is...

440
00:24:31,376 --> 00:24:33,844
Citizen Jury Trial.

441
00:24:34,813 --> 00:24:36,346
Are we on trial?

442
00:24:36,347 --> 00:24:38,449
Right. We're the ones being judged.

443
00:24:38,583 --> 00:24:40,418
Let's see what the rulebook says first.

444
00:24:40,618 --> 00:24:42,586
We're going to have to burn
some serious brainpower.

445
00:24:42,587 --> 00:24:44,188
I can already feel my brain
kicking into gear.

446
00:24:44,821 --> 00:24:48,725
Here are the rules
for the Citizen Jury Trial.

447
00:24:49,661 --> 00:24:55,499
Residents will take part
in a Citizen Jury Trial...

448
00:24:56,133 --> 00:24:58,835
before a panel of 33 jurors.

449
00:25:00,072 --> 00:25:03,440
The trial consists of two rounds.

450
00:25:04,141 --> 00:25:07,077
The leader will split all residents,

451
00:25:07,078 --> 00:25:09,579
excluding themselves, into two teams.

452
00:25:10,983 --> 00:25:14,217
Once set,
the rosters will remain locked...

453
00:25:14,218 --> 00:25:15,652
for both rounds.

454
00:25:16,355 --> 00:25:19,123
Each round will feature one case,

455
00:25:19,357 --> 00:25:22,092
for a total of two cases.

456
00:25:22,994 --> 00:25:25,363
For each case, both teams...

457
00:25:25,364 --> 00:25:27,598
will have 30 minutes
to present their arguments,

458
00:25:28,099 --> 00:25:31,201
followed by a one-hour voting window
for the jury.

459
00:25:32,037 --> 00:25:36,440
Each team may select
up to two representatives to speak.

460
00:25:36,941 --> 00:25:40,510
The remaining residents will use
this one-hour voting period...

461
00:25:40,746 --> 00:25:45,048
to lobby the jurors
and sway public opinion.

462
00:25:47,285 --> 00:25:50,821
At the end of each round,
the team that secures the jury vote...

463
00:25:51,255 --> 00:25:55,825
will be awarded 2,000 dollars
in personal funds per round.

464
00:25:56,394 --> 00:25:58,496
The losing team must select...

465
00:25:58,497 --> 00:26:01,031
one resident to evict per round.

466
00:26:03,401 --> 00:26:04,501
Per round?

467
00:26:04,736 --> 00:26:05,937
So two people total.

468
00:26:06,372 --> 00:26:07,771
I was stunned.

469
00:26:08,272 --> 00:26:11,207
We were being judged by outsiders,

470
00:26:11,709 --> 00:26:13,911
and people could actually
get eliminated over it.

471
00:26:14,045 --> 00:26:15,212
That's brutal.

472
00:26:15,880 --> 00:26:18,949
I've never actually handled
a citizen jury trial before.

473
00:26:18,950 --> 00:26:20,250
I know the concept.

474
00:26:20,251 --> 00:26:22,252
Citizen jury trials are fairly rare.

475
00:26:22,253 --> 00:26:25,622
They're usually reserved
for specific types of cases.

476
00:26:25,623 --> 00:26:28,425
They tend to be used
in cases like sex offenses,

477
00:26:28,426 --> 00:26:30,660
where a jury might bring
a different lens than career judges.

478
00:26:30,661 --> 00:26:33,196
But I'm the one who knows that world,
not Benjamin.

479
00:26:33,264 --> 00:26:35,265
Benjamin barely speaks Korean.

480
00:26:35,266 --> 00:26:36,833
This has nothing to do
with his background.

481
00:26:41,273 --> 00:26:44,574
- Please review the first case,
- Please.

482
00:26:44,575 --> 00:26:47,311
divide the residents
into the Defense and Prosecution teams,

483
00:26:47,312 --> 00:26:49,713
and predict the winning side.

484
00:26:50,281 --> 00:26:52,748
If the prediction is correct,

485
00:26:52,749 --> 00:26:56,253
the leader will receive 2,000 dollars.

486
00:26:57,588 --> 00:26:59,588
In this community court,

487
00:26:59,589 --> 00:27:02,424
the verdict will rest entirely...

488
00:27:02,425 --> 00:27:08,731
on the personal values
and moral judgment of the 33 jurors,

489
00:27:08,732 --> 00:27:10,867
regardless of what statutory law dictates.

490
00:27:11,303 --> 00:27:13,971
Please keep that in mind
as you make your case.

491
00:27:14,606 --> 00:27:16,306
Here is the first case.

492
00:27:16,747 --> 00:27:20,076
Reporter A was appalled
by the horrific conditions...

493
00:27:20,077 --> 00:27:22,479
sewing factory workers
were forced to endure,

494
00:27:22,880 --> 00:27:27,417
deprived of basic labor rights
and even the minimum wage.

495
00:27:27,586 --> 00:27:30,421
They wrote an in-depth piece
exposing the exploitation.

496
00:27:30,922 --> 00:27:33,357
However, during the investigation,

497
00:27:33,358 --> 00:27:36,293
the workers pleaded with them
not to run the story.

498
00:27:36,961 --> 00:27:40,864
"This is the only work we can get."

499
00:27:41,466 --> 00:27:45,335
"Please don't take our livelihoods away."

500
00:27:45,970 --> 00:27:48,237
After agonizing over it,

501
00:27:48,238 --> 00:27:50,207
Reporter A chose to kill the story...

502
00:27:50,709 --> 00:27:53,377
and look the other way.

503
00:27:54,612 --> 00:27:58,615
Is Reporter A guilty or not guilty?

504
00:28:00,585 --> 00:28:01,985
The core conflict comes down to this.

505
00:28:03,154 --> 00:28:04,856
"Please don't write about us."

506
00:28:05,656 --> 00:28:07,457
"Please don't take our jobs away."

507
00:28:07,992 --> 00:28:09,593
(Progressive)

508
00:28:09,594 --> 00:28:10,894
(Conservative)

509
00:28:10,895 --> 00:28:12,629
(Not guilty)

510
00:28:12,630 --> 00:28:14,564
(Guilty)

511
00:28:20,972 --> 00:28:23,073
(Rather than taking their jobs away,)

512
00:28:23,074 --> 00:28:25,275
(they buried the truth.)

513
00:28:29,747 --> 00:28:30,714
Upper-middle...

514
00:28:35,586 --> 00:28:36,619
Working-class...

515
00:28:45,730 --> 00:28:48,932
Gray, Superman, Ted, Nangja,
Banu, and Genie.

516
00:28:49,867 --> 00:28:52,202
Society needs real reform.

517
00:28:52,470 --> 00:28:55,472
Prosecution: guilty.

518
00:28:58,075 --> 00:29:01,277
I had to make this call
almost entirely on my own.

519
00:29:01,278 --> 00:29:03,212
Everyone's codes were revealed yesterday,

520
00:29:03,213 --> 00:29:05,815
and I had jotted every single one down.

521
00:29:05,816 --> 00:29:07,850
I went down the list,
checking who leaned left or right,

522
00:29:07,851 --> 00:29:11,354
and who came from an upper-middle
or working-class background.

523
00:29:11,355 --> 00:29:12,955
Based on that,

524
00:29:12,956 --> 00:29:15,191
I placed the more progressive residents
on the Prosecution Team,

525
00:29:15,192 --> 00:29:18,127
since they'd lean
toward reforming the system.

526
00:29:18,196 --> 00:29:21,765
And I put the right-leaning, working-class
residents on the Defense Team...

527
00:29:21,866 --> 00:29:24,435
believing they'd empathize more
with the workers' immediate survival...

528
00:29:24,436 --> 00:29:26,070
sharing a similar lived reality.

529
00:29:33,311 --> 00:29:34,778
(Polar Bear's final team selection)

530
00:29:34,779 --> 00:29:36,680
(Defense Team:
The reporter is not guilty.)

531
00:29:36,681 --> 00:29:38,649
(Prosecution Team:
The reporter is guilty.)

532
00:29:41,152 --> 00:29:43,620
(Defense Team)

533
00:29:44,055 --> 00:29:46,557
(Polar Bear predicted a victory
for the Defense Team.)

534
00:29:46,558 --> 00:29:48,291
Here she comes.

535
00:29:50,695 --> 00:29:52,530
- Polar Bear is out.
- Already?

536
00:29:52,531 --> 00:29:53,463
She's back.

537
00:29:53,831 --> 00:29:55,064
That took a while.

538
00:29:55,366 --> 00:29:57,134
Her heart must be so heavy right now.

539
00:30:02,306 --> 00:30:04,073
The Citizen Jury Trial...

540
00:30:04,675 --> 00:30:06,976
begins in ten minutes.

541
00:30:06,977 --> 00:30:08,745
- Ten?
- Ten minutes?

542
00:30:08,746 --> 00:30:09,912
Tell us the rosters first.

543
00:30:10,281 --> 00:30:12,849
- Teams are set.
- Okay.

544
00:30:12,850 --> 00:30:16,286
Michael, Gray, Ted, Banu,

545
00:30:16,287 --> 00:30:19,022
and Go Ae Shin are
on the Prosecution Team.

546
00:30:19,023 --> 00:30:24,029
Superman, Nangja, Dark Knight,
Benjamin, Sugar, and Genie...

547
00:30:24,030 --> 00:30:25,962
are on the Defense Team.

548
00:30:29,134 --> 00:30:31,669
To be honest, on our side we have Michael,

549
00:30:31,670 --> 00:30:33,836
Banu, Go Ae Shin,

550
00:30:34,239 --> 00:30:35,505
and myself.

551
00:30:35,773 --> 00:30:37,874
"Ted will carry the team."

552
00:30:37,875 --> 00:30:39,443
I could see it coming from a mile away.

553
00:30:39,811 --> 00:30:44,181
Here is your first case.

554
00:30:44,182 --> 00:30:46,516
This is the prompt you'll be debating.

555
00:30:46,884 --> 00:30:48,651
Isn't this right up your alley, Nangja?

556
00:30:48,676 --> 00:30:52,313
I'll probably rely more
on an emotional appeal than dry logic.

557
00:30:52,690 --> 00:30:54,324
The moment we received the prompt,

558
00:30:54,325 --> 00:30:57,961
it hit me so hard,
like it was my own life story.

559
00:30:57,962 --> 00:31:00,664
The topic struck a deeply personal chord.

560
00:31:00,665 --> 00:31:05,035
My perspective has always aligned on
with the working class.

561
00:31:05,036 --> 00:31:08,538
I felt I could bring genuine
emotional resonance to this argument.

562
00:31:08,806 --> 00:31:11,475
I'm also putting my faith in Benjamin.
What do you think?

563
00:31:11,709 --> 00:31:16,680
I think you'd do great
if we put you front and center.

564
00:31:20,284 --> 00:31:22,619
I'll give it a shot.

565
00:31:22,987 --> 00:31:23,853
All right.

566
00:31:27,825 --> 00:31:29,325
I'll make my point
and get out of the spotlight.

567
00:31:29,326 --> 00:31:32,228
Better to keep a low profile and lay low.

568
00:31:32,530 --> 00:31:33,396
We're running out of time.

569
00:31:33,397 --> 00:31:34,465
Eight minutes left.

570
00:31:34,665 --> 00:31:37,868
We need to blow them out of the water
without slipping up once.

571
00:31:38,069 --> 00:31:40,437
First off, here's our angle.

572
00:31:40,438 --> 00:31:42,506
The workers are saying,
"We need this money."

573
00:31:42,507 --> 00:31:44,241
"If you run this story,
we'll lose our livelihoods."

574
00:31:44,242 --> 00:31:45,885
We counter that turning a blind eye
out of sympathy...

575
00:31:45,910 --> 00:31:47,573
only creates more victims like them
down the road.

576
00:31:47,598 --> 00:31:51,148
For the sake of the next generation,
we have to intervene.

577
00:31:51,149 --> 00:31:54,084
Even if the journalist drafts the article,

578
00:31:54,085 --> 00:31:57,487
deciding whether to publish is ultimately
their editorial freedom.

579
00:31:57,488 --> 00:32:00,190
The other side will definitely push that.

580
00:32:00,191 --> 00:32:03,026
We shouldn't put the journalist
on trial here.

581
00:32:03,027 --> 00:32:05,228
Keep the spotlight on the workers.

582
00:32:05,229 --> 00:32:06,897
We need an emotional argument
rooted in their reality.

583
00:32:07,064 --> 00:32:07,930
The social safety net.

584
00:32:07,931 --> 00:32:09,665
We could even cite
what kebabmania82 wrote.

585
00:32:09,666 --> 00:32:11,367
People stay trapped in poverty
because they lack access to information.

586
00:32:11,368 --> 00:32:12,969
They don't know
what welfare programs exist.

587
00:32:12,970 --> 00:32:15,042
The reporter could have used the article
to connect them with those resources.

588
00:32:15,067 --> 00:32:16,048
Good point.

589
00:32:16,073 --> 00:32:17,907
Since Superman has a legal background,

590
00:32:17,908 --> 00:32:19,509
they might have a major edge.

591
00:32:19,510 --> 00:32:21,511
We have the upper hand on this one.

592
00:32:21,512 --> 00:32:25,515
We have so much emotional ground
to work with.

593
00:32:25,683 --> 00:32:27,984
There's an angle here
we can really capitalize on.

594
00:32:27,985 --> 00:32:30,887
The fact that they're a journalist
works heavily in our favor.

595
00:32:31,188 --> 00:32:34,257
Freedom of the press is
a cornerstone value.

596
00:32:34,258 --> 00:32:36,559
If a journalist investigates a story...

597
00:32:36,560 --> 00:32:38,628
out of a duty to do what's right,

598
00:32:38,629 --> 00:32:40,022
forcing them to kill it...

599
00:32:40,046 --> 00:32:41,964
is practically compelling them
to betray their conscience.

600
00:32:42,033 --> 00:32:44,901
I'll talk to as many jurors as possible...

601
00:32:44,902 --> 00:32:46,469
and sway their votes.

602
00:32:50,608 --> 00:32:54,010
The Citizen Jury Trial will begin shortly.

603
00:32:54,612 --> 00:32:59,382
All residents,
please assemble in the plaza.

604
00:32:59,383 --> 00:33:01,618
First impressions matter.

605
00:33:01,619 --> 00:33:03,053
I'm taking off the padded jacket.

606
00:33:03,054 --> 00:33:04,254
I was in sweatpants,

607
00:33:04,255 --> 00:33:06,056
but I changed
as soon as I saw them arrive.

608
00:33:06,057 --> 00:33:07,657
Nangja, take the lead on this.

609
00:33:07,658 --> 00:33:10,060
Speak from the heart and move them.

610
00:33:10,061 --> 00:33:11,661
This is your stage.

611
00:33:11,662 --> 00:33:13,130
- Let's do this.
- For real.

612
00:33:13,397 --> 00:33:14,832
Beyond a reasonable doubt...

613
00:33:16,033 --> 00:33:17,567
This is intense. Hold on.

614
00:33:19,170 --> 00:33:21,805
(The jurors are members
of a university debate club...)

615
00:33:21,806 --> 00:33:23,406
(called "Seron.")

616
00:33:23,407 --> 00:33:25,075
What's going on?

617
00:33:25,509 --> 00:33:27,443
- Hello.
- Hello.

618
00:33:27,444 --> 00:33:28,578
- Hello.
- Hello.

619
00:33:31,882 --> 00:33:32,950
Go get them, Benjamin.

620
00:33:33,084 --> 00:33:33,917
Let's do this.

621
00:33:33,918 --> 00:33:36,153
Time to load your Korean language patch!

622
00:33:36,154 --> 00:33:38,188
- Already installed.
- Switching to native Korean mode.

623
00:33:38,189 --> 00:33:39,756
Benjamin is speaking for their side.

624
00:33:39,757 --> 00:33:41,491
Benjamin and Nangja are arguing the case.

625
00:33:41,492 --> 00:33:43,960
What if I take the podium alone,
while the rest of you work the floor?

626
00:33:43,961 --> 00:33:45,128
That works.

627
00:33:45,463 --> 00:33:48,765
That might actually draw
more sympathy from the jury.

628
00:33:49,934 --> 00:33:51,600
Classic Ted style.

629
00:33:51,601 --> 00:33:55,237
He could have just said,
"Gray, I've got this on my own."

630
00:33:55,238 --> 00:33:58,607
"Gray, wouldn't it be more effective..."

631
00:33:58,608 --> 00:34:01,978
"for you to be on the floor
persuading jurors?"

632
00:34:01,979 --> 00:34:03,781
He was basically telling me
to sit this one out.

633
00:34:08,719 --> 00:34:12,388
The Citizen Jury Trial will now begin.

634
00:34:13,357 --> 00:34:15,292
Once the moderator finishes speaking,

635
00:34:15,626 --> 00:34:19,262
the first round will run for 30 minutes.

636
00:34:20,397 --> 00:34:22,231
Hello, jurors.

637
00:34:22,232 --> 00:34:25,267
I'm Polar Bear, the Lifetime Leader...

638
00:34:25,268 --> 00:34:27,370
of The Community: Tension Under One Roof.

639
00:34:27,371 --> 00:34:28,371
Welcome.

640
00:34:31,709 --> 00:34:33,977
Allow me to introduce the first case...

641
00:34:33,978 --> 00:34:35,679
for today's trial.

642
00:34:35,680 --> 00:34:38,901
"Reporter A was appalled
by the horrific conditions..."

643
00:34:38,926 --> 00:34:41,485
"sewing factory workers
were forced to endure,"

644
00:34:41,510 --> 00:34:45,689
"deprived of basic labor rights
and even the minimum wage."

645
00:34:45,690 --> 00:34:47,857
"They wrote an in-depth piece
exposing the exploitation."

646
00:34:47,858 --> 00:34:51,461
"However, the workers pleaded with them
not to run the story."

647
00:34:51,462 --> 00:34:54,864
"They begged the reporter
not to take away their livelihoods."

648
00:34:55,366 --> 00:34:58,301
"Reporter A ultimately chose
to look the other way."

649
00:34:58,602 --> 00:35:02,472
"Is Reporter A guilty or not guilty?"

650
00:35:03,073 --> 00:35:05,875
We will hear from both sides,
the Prosecution and the Defense.

651
00:35:06,043 --> 00:35:08,678
First, the Prosecution Team.

652
00:35:08,679 --> 00:35:09,779
("The reporter was wrong
to kill the story.")

653
00:35:09,780 --> 00:35:11,315
(Go Ae Shin, Banu, Gray, Ted, Michael)

654
00:35:11,316 --> 00:35:14,384
And representing the Defense Team.

655
00:35:14,385 --> 00:35:15,451
("The reporter is not guilty.")

656
00:35:15,452 --> 00:35:17,320
(Benjamin, Nangja, Dark Knight,
Superman, Sugar, Genie)

657
00:35:20,758 --> 00:35:23,626
With that, opening arguments may begin.

658
00:35:23,894 --> 00:35:28,698
Hello, I'm Benjamin
representing the Defense.

659
00:35:28,699 --> 00:35:33,302
Korean isn't my first language,
so please bear with me.

660
00:35:33,904 --> 00:35:35,906
Is putting him on the spot
part of their play?

661
00:35:35,907 --> 00:35:36,739
Yep.

662
00:35:36,740 --> 00:35:38,207
("The reporter is not guilty.")

663
00:35:38,208 --> 00:35:42,144
It wasn't the journalist
who exploited these laborers.

664
00:35:43,079 --> 00:35:44,848
All the journalist did...

665
00:35:45,017 --> 00:35:49,118
was make an editorial decision
on whether to publish.

666
00:35:49,853 --> 00:35:51,821
Freedom of speech is guaranteed...

667
00:35:51,822 --> 00:35:53,989
in both the US and Korea.

668
00:35:54,191 --> 00:35:58,795
Compelling a journalist to publish simply
because they investigated a story...

669
00:35:59,230 --> 00:36:02,399
strikes at the heart
of our most fundamental liberties.

670
00:36:02,900 --> 00:36:06,936
Freedom of speech. Freedom of the press.

671
00:36:07,838 --> 00:36:10,740
We argue that the reporter shirked...

672
00:36:11,151 --> 00:36:16,146
our generation's moral obligation
to build a better future for the next.

673
00:36:16,647 --> 00:36:18,982
Consider the facts before us.

674
00:36:18,983 --> 00:36:21,718
We aren't condemning a journalist
who stood by and did nothing.

675
00:36:21,819 --> 00:36:24,364
They went out of their way to investigate
and write the piece,

676
00:36:24,389 --> 00:36:26,265
only to bury it at the finish line.

677
00:36:26,290 --> 00:36:27,790
Simply because the workers
they interviewed...

678
00:36:27,791 --> 00:36:30,193
pleaded with them to drop it
to save their jobs.

679
00:36:30,194 --> 00:36:32,662
Do the few workers
the reporter spoke to...

680
00:36:32,663 --> 00:36:34,998
truly speak for an entire workforce?

681
00:36:34,999 --> 00:36:36,432
That is the central question.

682
00:36:36,634 --> 00:36:38,969
This trial may not follow
the strict letter of statutory law.

683
00:36:38,970 --> 00:36:42,004
Yet the premise clearly notes
that these workers lacked minimum wage...

684
00:36:42,029 --> 00:36:43,374
and basic labor protections.

685
00:36:43,474 --> 00:36:45,308
That presupposes our legal framework,

686
00:36:45,309 --> 00:36:47,611
with minimum-wage regulations
and labor standards, already exists.

687
00:36:48,285 --> 00:36:51,048
Which means institutional safeguards exist
to protect them.

688
00:36:51,148 --> 00:36:53,649
The reporter chose to bury the truth...

689
00:36:53,674 --> 00:36:58,022
out of personal pity
for the individuals in front of them.

690
00:36:58,061 --> 00:37:01,725
They also failed in their duty
to inform the broader public...

691
00:37:01,726 --> 00:37:04,193
that these legal protections exist.

692
00:37:04,194 --> 00:37:08,065
Someone has to break the cycle
and advocate for the next generation,

693
00:37:08,332 --> 00:37:12,035
yet they chose short-term comfort
for the few in their immediate view.

694
00:37:12,136 --> 00:37:14,638
We hold that this constitutes a failure
of journalistic duty.

695
00:37:16,473 --> 00:37:18,142
I couldn't relate to a single word.

696
00:37:18,609 --> 00:37:21,911
He spoke so eloquently
and laid out a flawless argument.

697
00:37:22,112 --> 00:37:24,780
A utopian world where nobody starves...

698
00:37:25,015 --> 00:37:26,650
and the state solves every problem.

699
00:37:27,151 --> 00:37:29,986
Is a journalist meant to blast
every detail into the public domain...

700
00:37:29,987 --> 00:37:33,556
and lay raw lives bare,
consequences be damned?

701
00:37:33,625 --> 00:37:36,026
It works in theory,

702
00:37:36,327 --> 00:37:37,427
but reality isn't a textbook.

703
00:37:37,996 --> 00:37:40,930
I want to share a bit of my own story...

704
00:37:40,931 --> 00:37:42,965
from when I was growing up.

705
00:37:43,567 --> 00:37:44,267
I wasn't...

706
00:37:44,268 --> 00:37:45,835
("The reporter is not guilty.")

707
00:37:45,836 --> 00:37:48,103
exactly born with a silver spoon.

708
00:37:48,104 --> 00:37:51,608
I grew up with very little.

709
00:37:51,609 --> 00:37:55,241
My generation bore the full brunt
of the IMF crisis...

710
00:37:55,266 --> 00:37:57,046
during our middle and high school years.

711
00:37:57,047 --> 00:38:00,316
All of a sudden, if I wanted to study,

712
00:38:00,551 --> 00:38:04,154
I had to take night shifts
just to afford workbooks.

713
00:38:04,589 --> 00:38:07,990
As you know, legal job options...

714
00:38:07,991 --> 00:38:09,726
for minors are practically nonexistent.

715
00:38:09,826 --> 00:38:13,362
My first gig was the graveyard shift
at a convenience store.

716
00:38:13,797 --> 00:38:15,698
Working those nights,

717
00:38:16,501 --> 00:38:18,968
I had to deal
with some terrifying customers.

718
00:38:19,370 --> 00:38:20,169
Honestly.

719
00:38:20,170 --> 00:38:21,570
("The reporter is not guilty.")

720
00:38:21,571 --> 00:38:23,839
I had no work experience,

721
00:38:23,840 --> 00:38:25,909
so my pay was shorted constantly
without me even realizing it.

722
00:38:25,910 --> 00:38:28,778
Yet because I was working illegally,

723
00:38:28,779 --> 00:38:30,980
I couldn't demand my rights.

724
00:38:31,180 --> 00:38:33,681
After that, I found work
at a fried chicken joint.

725
00:38:33,917 --> 00:38:35,718
Back then, at 24-hour spots,

726
00:38:35,719 --> 00:38:39,288
the graveyard shift was filled
with people like me,

727
00:38:39,289 --> 00:38:42,858
underage kids working under the table...

728
00:38:43,127 --> 00:38:46,162
or migrant workers.

729
00:38:46,163 --> 00:38:51,067
I spent entire nights skewering meat
alongside Ukrainian exchange students...

730
00:38:51,068 --> 00:38:53,570
who had come to Korea to study.

731
00:38:54,437 --> 00:38:56,639
I'd head out at 10 p.m...

732
00:38:56,640 --> 00:38:58,174
and work until 3 a.m.

733
00:38:58,442 --> 00:39:00,777
I'd clock out, head home,

734
00:39:00,778 --> 00:39:02,478
and go straight to school.

735
00:39:03,547 --> 00:39:06,883
Then one day, the owner told me
not to come in anymore.

736
00:39:07,584 --> 00:39:08,651
When I asked why,

737
00:39:09,185 --> 00:39:11,721
he had the nerve to say
it didn't sit right with his conscience...

738
00:39:11,722 --> 00:39:13,289
to exploit a young student like this.

739
00:39:13,676 --> 00:39:15,111
After making me work up to that very day.

740
00:39:15,259 --> 00:39:18,395
I needed to work one more shift
to receive my payout.

741
00:39:18,562 --> 00:39:21,664
In the end, I walked away with nothing.

742
00:39:22,200 --> 00:39:25,668
And I couldn't say a single word.

743
00:39:28,131 --> 00:39:29,766
The reason I'm laying all this out is,

744
00:39:29,907 --> 00:39:33,510
honestly, that I hear the noble rhetoric
from the other team.

745
00:39:33,511 --> 00:39:36,379
But none of it touches real life.

746
00:39:37,214 --> 00:39:40,016
On paper, yes, we shouldn't let
this cycle of exploitation...

747
00:39:40,017 --> 00:39:41,451
pass down to the next generation.

748
00:39:41,552 --> 00:39:44,973
Then where do the workers
in that sewing factory...

749
00:39:44,998 --> 00:39:46,121
go tonight?

750
00:39:47,591 --> 00:39:49,725
If that journalist exposes the place,

751
00:39:49,726 --> 00:39:51,594
the factory closes its doors tomorrow,

752
00:39:51,928 --> 00:39:54,564
and those workers can't put food
on the table tonight.

753
00:39:55,166 --> 00:39:57,934
They can't make rent this month.

754
00:39:58,535 --> 00:39:59,670
Where are they supposed to turn?

755
00:40:00,234 --> 00:40:01,987
Will you step in
and take responsibility for their lives?

756
00:40:03,240 --> 00:40:04,807
Who breaks the cycle,

757
00:40:04,808 --> 00:40:06,710
and when does that relief actually arrive?

758
00:40:07,110 --> 00:40:09,845
They don't even have dinner tonight.

759
00:40:13,150 --> 00:40:17,620
It's so easy to preach ideals.

760
00:40:18,322 --> 00:40:20,699
Everyone has the right answers,
but nobody provides immediate answers.

761
00:40:20,724 --> 00:40:22,359
Who doesn't know
what the ideal world looks like?

762
00:40:25,362 --> 00:40:27,030
Even throughout college,

763
00:40:27,297 --> 00:40:30,701
I survived on two triangle gimbap a day.

764
00:40:31,635 --> 00:40:34,537
One for lunch, one for dinner.

765
00:40:35,952 --> 00:40:37,437
Whenever I could barely
stomach another bite,

766
00:40:37,462 --> 00:40:38,909
I'd treat myself to a single can of tuna.

767
00:40:39,543 --> 00:40:41,044
So what crime did that reporter
actually commit?

768
00:40:41,345 --> 00:40:43,380
What do you think was
tearing them apart inside?

769
00:40:45,082 --> 00:40:46,716
Speaking from lived experience,

770
00:40:47,017 --> 00:40:50,519
I genuinely want to ask you that.

771
00:40:55,626 --> 00:40:59,062
I don't know why the tears wouldn't stop.

772
00:40:59,630 --> 00:41:01,464
I've never...

773
00:41:02,032 --> 00:41:05,167
walked in her shoes,

774
00:41:05,296 --> 00:41:08,661
so I don't know if I have any right...

775
00:41:08,693 --> 00:41:11,531
to say I understand what she went through.

776
00:41:13,211 --> 00:41:14,711
It just overwhelmed me.

777
00:41:14,712 --> 00:41:16,179
(Genie: right, feminism,
upper-middle, conservative)

778
00:41:16,280 --> 00:41:20,783
I understand that people endure
the very pain you described.

779
00:41:20,884 --> 00:41:23,052
Yet in such a short time,

780
00:41:23,486 --> 00:41:25,855
the world has progressed
and continues to change...

781
00:41:25,856 --> 00:41:28,057
precisely because people refuse
to stop sounding the alarm.

782
00:41:28,058 --> 00:41:29,425
Internet comments don't correct
these injustices.

783
00:41:29,426 --> 00:41:31,761
Journalists dragged these abuses
into the light of day.

784
00:41:31,762 --> 00:41:32,928
("The reporter is guilty.")

785
00:41:32,929 --> 00:41:34,964
Not having an immediate solution
doesn't give you license to look away.

786
00:41:34,965 --> 00:41:36,933
Shouldn't that journalist have run
the exposé and stayed committed...

787
00:41:36,958 --> 00:41:38,835
to fighting for a resolution,
no matter how many years it took?

788
00:41:39,536 --> 00:41:40,503
This person is a journalist.

789
00:41:40,504 --> 00:41:44,206
Killing the story under the guise
of protecting the vulnerable...

790
00:41:44,207 --> 00:41:45,951
is nothing more
than burying systemic injustice.

791
00:41:45,976 --> 00:41:47,870
A journalist cannot compromise
their integrity like that.

792
00:41:47,894 --> 00:41:49,012
It's fundamentally wrong.

793
00:41:49,013 --> 00:41:51,380
We must set a precedent
for those who come after us.

794
00:41:51,381 --> 00:41:52,214
Thank you.

795
00:41:53,150 --> 00:41:57,620
That concludes the open debate
between both sides.

796
00:41:57,621 --> 00:41:59,622
From this point,
residents have one hour...

797
00:42:00,157 --> 00:42:01,824
to engage freely with the jury panel.

798
00:42:04,494 --> 00:42:06,295
(Even after the debate ends,)

799
00:42:06,296 --> 00:42:07,262
It's all right.

800
00:42:07,287 --> 00:42:09,604
(Genie remains visibly shaken.)

801
00:42:11,034 --> 00:42:12,801
What's going on with me?

802
00:42:12,802 --> 00:42:13,669
Hey.

803
00:42:14,605 --> 00:42:15,538
We'll be back.

804
00:42:16,540 --> 00:42:19,375
I'm sorry. I'm not trying to be
over the top.

805
00:42:19,376 --> 00:42:22,044
I can't even begin to imagine
what you went through.

806
00:42:22,045 --> 00:42:23,813
- It's okay.
- I'm sorry I'm being ridiculous.

807
00:42:24,214 --> 00:42:26,314
I caused this. I'll disappear for a bit.

808
00:42:26,315 --> 00:42:27,583
I argued the case, so I can go in.

809
00:42:27,584 --> 00:42:28,484
Goodness.

810
00:42:37,761 --> 00:42:39,361
The jury voting will now begin...

811
00:42:39,996 --> 00:42:42,097
and continue for one hour.

812
00:42:42,934 --> 00:42:45,468
Jurors, please feel free...

813
00:42:45,469 --> 00:42:46,936
to visit the Community Center...

814
00:42:47,204 --> 00:42:48,971
and cast your vote.

815
00:42:50,675 --> 00:42:52,341
(Jury persuasion begins.)

816
00:42:52,342 --> 00:42:53,609
Hello.

817
00:42:54,011 --> 00:42:56,479
If you think the reporter is guilty,

818
00:42:56,480 --> 00:42:58,380
let's talk about it together.

819
00:42:58,381 --> 00:43:01,250
What do you guys think?

820
00:43:01,251 --> 00:43:02,986
- We think they're innocent.
- You think they're innocent?

821
00:43:02,987 --> 00:43:05,421
Then I'd like to hear what you think.

822
00:43:05,422 --> 00:43:07,457
Should the person who's involved...

823
00:43:07,458 --> 00:43:08,992
have any effect
on whether a story gets published?

824
00:43:08,993 --> 00:43:11,728
If a reporter only publishes a story
when they think something is wrong,

825
00:43:11,729 --> 00:43:14,297
but kills the story when they decide
there's no wrongdoing,

826
00:43:14,298 --> 00:43:15,965
I think that's wrong...

827
00:43:15,966 --> 00:43:19,235
when it comes
to the public's right to know.

828
00:43:19,236 --> 00:43:20,269
(Guilty: The Reporter is guilty.)

829
00:43:20,270 --> 00:43:22,038
I'd like to push back on that point.

830
00:43:22,039 --> 00:43:23,807
All right. Let's discuss it.

831
00:43:23,808 --> 00:43:26,943
This wasn't a decision...

832
00:43:26,944 --> 00:43:29,445
the reporter made entirely on their own.

833
00:43:29,713 --> 00:43:32,648
It was a direct request
from the people directly affected.

834
00:43:32,649 --> 00:43:35,385
If you keep accommodating
requests like this,

835
00:43:35,386 --> 00:43:38,054
wouldn't that make
media collusion more likely?

836
00:43:38,055 --> 00:43:41,057
If the request had come...

837
00:43:41,058 --> 00:43:43,492
from the owner of the factory,

838
00:43:43,493 --> 00:43:45,428
then that could potentially turn
into collusion.

839
00:43:45,429 --> 00:43:47,496
But that's not what happened here.

840
00:43:47,497 --> 00:43:49,965
These workers were worried
about their livelihoods.

841
00:43:49,966 --> 00:43:51,400
I agree that ethically,

842
00:43:51,401 --> 00:43:53,669
the reporter should have reported it.

843
00:43:53,704 --> 00:43:56,439
But I'm not sure
that means they're guilty.

844
00:43:56,440 --> 00:43:59,542
Isn't failing to report a crime
a crime in itself?

845
00:43:59,743 --> 00:44:00,543
That's aiding and abetting.

846
00:44:00,811 --> 00:44:02,411
- You're right.
- Exactly.

847
00:44:02,546 --> 00:44:04,313
By reporting it,

848
00:44:04,314 --> 00:44:07,016
we can move toward a better society.

849
00:44:07,150 --> 00:44:08,851
"A better world" is...

850
00:44:08,852 --> 00:44:11,954
an abstract goal
that no one can really define.

851
00:44:11,955 --> 00:44:15,124
So you can't hold someone
legally responsible...

852
00:44:15,125 --> 00:44:17,359
for failing to make
the world a better place.

853
00:44:17,527 --> 00:44:19,361
These things keep happening...

854
00:44:19,362 --> 00:44:21,397
because we keep turning a blind eye.

855
00:44:21,398 --> 00:44:23,099
Employers exploit workers
by taking advantage of the fact...

856
00:44:23,100 --> 00:44:24,834
that they feel like they have
no other options.

857
00:44:24,835 --> 00:44:27,903
That's how they get away
with paying people less than minimum wage.

858
00:44:28,172 --> 00:44:31,373
If we want to change that,
these issues need to be exposed.

859
00:44:31,374 --> 00:44:32,842
Imagine you need to make money
just to get by.

860
00:44:33,176 --> 00:44:35,178
If you don't earn any money today,
your family could starve.

861
00:44:35,179 --> 00:44:36,478
There's a system...

862
00:44:36,479 --> 00:44:39,615
that provides unemployment benefits
for up to six months,

863
00:44:39,616 --> 00:44:40,783
even if you quit your job.

864
00:44:40,784 --> 00:44:42,918
So there are definitely ways...

865
00:44:42,919 --> 00:44:46,822
to help people get other jobs
through programs like that.

866
00:44:46,823 --> 00:44:49,224
Maybe calling it armchair theorizing...

867
00:44:49,225 --> 00:44:51,326
might be a little too harsh,

868
00:44:51,561 --> 00:44:55,130
but it's crucial to make sure people can
survive right now.

869
00:44:55,131 --> 00:44:56,398
Someone said something very hopeful.

870
00:44:56,800 --> 00:44:58,066
That they can receive...

871
00:44:58,568 --> 00:45:01,036
unemployment benefits for six months
and then find another job.

872
00:45:01,037 --> 00:45:02,370
(The reporter is innocent.)

873
00:45:02,371 --> 00:45:04,573
How easy is it really for someone...

874
00:45:04,574 --> 00:45:06,942
who's starting from rock bottom
to find their next job?

875
00:45:07,210 --> 00:45:10,778
My financial situation in Iran
suddenly got worse,

876
00:45:10,779 --> 00:45:13,115
so I was relying on my parents.

877
00:45:13,416 --> 00:45:16,452
Then they couldn't support me anymore,
so I had to get a part-time job.

878
00:45:16,453 --> 00:45:18,353
I came to Korea when I was a minor.

879
00:45:18,354 --> 00:45:20,055
I've worked
at a fried chicken restaurant too,

880
00:45:20,056 --> 00:45:22,024
so I really related to her story.

881
00:45:22,025 --> 00:45:23,458
"I'm a person just like everyone else,"

882
00:45:23,459 --> 00:45:26,228
"so why couldn't I even get minimum wage?"

883
00:45:26,229 --> 00:45:28,831
I'm a foreigner, but I'm still a person,
just like everyone else.

884
00:45:28,832 --> 00:45:30,899
We're all human beings.

885
00:45:31,368 --> 00:45:33,069
That really matters to me.

886
00:45:33,070 --> 00:45:36,039
Shouldn't we be looking for ways
to address the problem at its root?

887
00:45:36,040 --> 00:45:37,740
But I don't understand...

888
00:45:37,741 --> 00:45:39,675
why we place that responsibility solely
on reporters.

889
00:45:39,676 --> 00:45:40,810
We're not saying "solely on reporters."

890
00:45:40,811 --> 00:45:42,978
We're saying the reporter has
a responsibility too.

891
00:45:43,446 --> 00:45:45,013
Wouldn't publishing the story create...

892
00:45:45,014 --> 00:45:47,049
a kind of butterfly effect?

893
00:45:47,050 --> 00:45:49,317
The butterfly effect is nice. Sure.

894
00:45:49,318 --> 00:45:51,353
Maybe all these smart people
will make things better.

895
00:45:51,888 --> 00:45:53,655
If the story were published,

896
00:45:53,857 --> 00:45:55,592
would the factory owner apologize
to the workers?

897
00:45:55,593 --> 00:45:57,227
Or would he be more likely to say,
"You're fired."

898
00:45:57,361 --> 00:45:58,761
"I'll hire someone else..."

899
00:45:58,762 --> 00:46:00,563
"and actually pay them properly?"

900
00:46:00,798 --> 00:46:03,166
You know what life is like.
Which one is more realistic?

901
00:46:05,001 --> 00:46:07,603
(The jury vote begins.)

902
00:46:07,938 --> 00:46:09,872
(Innocent)

903
00:46:09,873 --> 00:46:13,543
(Please vote for the side you agree with.)

904
00:46:14,144 --> 00:46:17,613
(Innocent, guilty, innocent, guilty)

905
00:46:20,016 --> 00:46:21,783
How torn those people must be.

906
00:46:23,620 --> 00:46:25,421
Actually, I feel like...

907
00:46:26,891 --> 00:46:28,324
these codes don't mean anything.

908
00:46:29,192 --> 00:46:31,593
Genie is at a three
on the upper-middle side.

909
00:46:33,029 --> 00:46:34,496
That means nothing.

910
00:46:37,701 --> 00:46:39,669
Listening to Nangja's speech...

911
00:46:39,670 --> 00:46:41,070
made me think.

912
00:46:41,738 --> 00:46:44,406
We all gathered to build a community,

913
00:46:45,019 --> 00:46:46,641
but all we talk about is the spy.

914
00:46:47,611 --> 00:46:49,878
We've been given...

915
00:46:50,347 --> 00:46:51,447
this space and time...

916
00:46:51,448 --> 00:46:54,651
to really talk
about each other's worldviews,

917
00:46:54,652 --> 00:46:57,053
but I feel like we're not making
the most of it.

918
00:46:57,054 --> 00:46:58,788
That's something
I've been thinking about today...

919
00:46:58,789 --> 00:47:00,289
as the leader.

920
00:47:01,658 --> 00:47:03,659
By the way, could we ask this question?

921
00:47:04,327 --> 00:47:06,461
Michael mentioned it briefly last night.

922
00:47:07,297 --> 00:47:10,366
Whether you can attack two people
in one round?

923
00:47:11,067 --> 00:47:13,068
I thought it was capped at one per round.

924
00:47:13,069 --> 00:47:14,603
Could I be wrong?

925
00:47:14,773 --> 00:47:16,239
Wait! Then...

926
00:47:16,240 --> 00:47:18,908
you could go all-in on the third round...

927
00:47:19,276 --> 00:47:21,344
and attack as much as you want.

928
00:47:22,145 --> 00:47:24,146
It kept nagging...

929
00:47:25,248 --> 00:47:26,982
at me yesterday.

930
00:47:27,625 --> 00:47:31,394
Why is it 2 people, not 1 or 3?

931
00:47:32,188 --> 00:47:35,524
Why did they get it wrong?
They must know the answer.

932
00:47:35,525 --> 00:47:37,259
- They knew they could eliminate them.
- Why?

933
00:47:37,727 --> 00:47:39,395
Since they failed in Round Two,

934
00:47:39,396 --> 00:47:41,296
I don't think it was the spy.

935
00:47:41,297 --> 00:47:42,464
The spy wouldn't have failed.

936
00:47:43,133 --> 00:47:45,401
So at the end,

937
00:47:45,402 --> 00:47:47,002
it suddenly hit me.

938
00:47:47,570 --> 00:47:48,637
It wasn't the spy.

939
00:47:50,140 --> 00:47:51,073
It wasn't the spy.

940
00:47:51,708 --> 00:47:52,975
That's a problem...

941
00:47:53,777 --> 00:47:56,479
because they pretended
to be part of the community...

942
00:47:57,480 --> 00:47:59,481
and eliminated someone unnecessarily,

943
00:47:59,506 --> 00:48:00,840
which makes them even worse.

944
00:48:04,721 --> 00:48:07,790
By Superman's definition,
they're basically a traitor.

945
00:48:07,791 --> 00:48:09,358
They're a traitor.

946
00:48:09,619 --> 00:48:10,719
Traitors are...

947
00:48:11,594 --> 00:48:13,962
worse than the spy because they don't
have to eliminate someone.

948
00:48:13,963 --> 00:48:14,896
Right.

949
00:48:15,332 --> 00:48:16,666
How can we find out who did it?

950
00:48:16,667 --> 00:48:18,467
We could ask a question
to narrow people down.

951
00:48:18,468 --> 00:48:20,202
"Who tried an ideology check?"

952
00:48:20,203 --> 00:48:21,070
"Who..."

953
00:48:22,445 --> 00:48:24,379
"has the most money?"

954
00:48:26,176 --> 00:48:27,042
Gray.

955
00:48:27,477 --> 00:48:30,612
(Currently in first place
for personal funds)

956
00:48:30,613 --> 00:48:31,747
Why did he eliminate her?

957
00:48:33,883 --> 00:48:35,584
"Who has the most money?"

958
00:48:37,854 --> 00:48:39,722
- Right!
- That's crazy.

959
00:48:39,723 --> 00:48:41,224
They might not tell us,
but that's "information."

960
00:48:41,225 --> 00:48:42,625
- We should try that.
- We could try that.

961
00:48:42,626 --> 00:48:43,892
I should write that down.

962
00:48:44,160 --> 00:48:46,161
We could catch either of them.

963
00:48:46,162 --> 00:48:47,195
We could.

964
00:48:47,331 --> 00:48:49,398
Either a spy or a traitor.

965
00:48:51,307 --> 00:48:52,674
That works in my favor.

966
00:48:53,062 --> 00:48:54,462
Now people are focusing on them.

967
00:48:54,705 --> 00:48:56,138
I nudged them...

968
00:48:56,139 --> 00:48:58,207
toward finding out who had the most money.

969
00:48:58,475 --> 00:49:00,110
Nangja and I already made a deal.

970
00:49:00,111 --> 00:49:01,911
She just played it out in front of them.

971
00:49:01,912 --> 00:49:05,347
It's not that bad.
If we had to eliminate someone,

972
00:49:05,348 --> 00:49:06,950
I don't think there'd be much pushback...

973
00:49:06,951 --> 00:49:09,151
if I picked Gray.

974
00:49:09,919 --> 00:49:12,522
Freedom of the press is
a fundamental constitutional value...

975
00:49:12,523 --> 00:49:15,957
that is commonly recognized everywhere,
as long as it's not a dictatorship.

976
00:49:16,326 --> 00:49:17,460
I don't think...

977
00:49:17,461 --> 00:49:20,529
any professional code of ethics should
override a person's freedom of conscience.

978
00:49:20,530 --> 00:49:21,997
When you look at it as a conflict
between two values,

979
00:49:21,998 --> 00:49:23,933
I think they're pretty evenly matched.

980
00:49:23,934 --> 00:49:26,802
That standard is different for everyone.

981
00:49:26,803 --> 00:49:28,637
You can't just impose
your own ethical standards on someone...

982
00:49:28,638 --> 00:49:30,372
and declare that they're in the wrong.

983
00:49:30,440 --> 00:49:32,408
If I were a truly ethical reporter,

984
00:49:32,475 --> 00:49:34,978
I would have tried to tell the story...

985
00:49:34,979 --> 00:49:36,412
in a way that made people feel
for these workers.

986
00:49:36,413 --> 00:49:37,746
I would've written...

987
00:49:37,881 --> 00:49:39,080
a really compelling, emotional story...

988
00:49:39,081 --> 00:49:40,715
and gotten it out to the media.

989
00:49:40,716 --> 00:49:42,817
I'd even get my reporter friends involved.

990
00:49:42,818 --> 00:49:45,052
I absolutely agree that reporters have...

991
00:49:45,053 --> 00:49:48,490
a huge responsibility
to make the world a better place.

992
00:49:48,491 --> 00:49:52,694
I think you're underestimating
how serious labor exploitation is.

993
00:49:52,695 --> 00:49:54,396
Of course working conditions
need to improve.

994
00:49:54,397 --> 00:49:57,866
But that won't happen overnight.
People can lose their jobs overnight,

995
00:49:58,168 --> 00:50:00,202
but they can change everything
with the stroke of a pen.

996
00:50:00,603 --> 00:50:01,937
To get out of a system...

997
00:50:01,938 --> 00:50:04,806
that treats you like garbage,

998
00:50:05,174 --> 00:50:08,042
you have to stay up all night
working your butt off for a way out.

999
00:50:08,043 --> 00:50:09,244
That's more realistic...

1000
00:50:09,846 --> 00:50:12,147
than just sitting around waiting
for the law to change.

1001
00:50:12,615 --> 00:50:14,983
Personally, I completely understood
how they would feel,

1002
00:50:14,984 --> 00:50:16,318
- Right.
- and it really moved me.

1003
00:50:16,854 --> 00:50:18,854
But is this the right thing to do
in terms of social progress?

1004
00:50:19,156 --> 00:50:21,156
Listen.
Why do you think it's a personal issue?

1005
00:50:21,424 --> 00:50:23,725
How many people live
an ordinary life like me?

1006
00:50:23,927 --> 00:50:25,394
- Right.
- What percentage of people live like me?

1007
00:50:25,395 --> 00:50:27,196
Are there more rich people
or more poor people?

1008
00:50:27,923 --> 00:50:28,997
How is this just personal?

1009
00:50:29,566 --> 00:50:30,500
Think about it.

1010
00:50:30,501 --> 00:50:32,235
All my friends grew up the same way.

1011
00:50:32,236 --> 00:50:33,688
Why are you calling that
a personal experience?

1012
00:50:33,689 --> 00:50:34,870
(The reporter is innocent.)

1013
00:50:35,305 --> 00:50:37,906
Voting will close in five minutes.

1014
00:50:45,849 --> 00:50:47,650
(All 33 jurors have cast their votes.)

1015
00:50:47,651 --> 00:50:49,819
I voted guilty.

1016
00:50:49,820 --> 00:50:52,054
After listening to both sides,

1017
00:50:52,055 --> 00:50:55,791
I thought there was something wrong
with their sense of professional ethics,

1018
00:50:55,792 --> 00:50:59,261
and that they were in the wrong
as a reporter.

1019
00:50:59,262 --> 00:51:00,763
I voted for the Defense Team.

1020
00:51:01,164 --> 00:51:03,932
Since the victims didn't want the story
to be made public,

1021
00:51:03,933 --> 00:51:06,668
I felt that respecting their wishes was
the most important thing.

1022
00:51:06,669 --> 00:51:08,203
I voted guilty.

1023
00:51:08,204 --> 00:51:11,340
Banu's argument resonated
with me the most.

1024
00:51:11,641 --> 00:51:13,775
Nangja's argument lacked logic.

1025
00:51:13,776 --> 00:51:17,112
I understand why she felt
that way emotionally,

1026
00:51:17,780 --> 00:51:20,949
but personally, I don't think many people
would be persuaded by that alone.

1027
00:51:21,719 --> 00:51:23,719
Voting has now closed.

1028
00:51:24,954 --> 00:51:27,255
The results are being tallied.

1029
00:51:27,757 --> 00:51:30,092
All residents,

1030
00:51:30,093 --> 00:51:31,927
please stand by inside the house.

1031
00:51:32,128 --> 00:51:35,598
(Round 1 of the Citizen Jury Trial ends.)

1032
00:51:35,866 --> 00:51:38,000
- Are you upset?
- No.

1033
00:51:38,001 --> 00:51:39,435
Good work, everyone.

1034
00:51:40,871 --> 00:51:42,137
- Great work.
- I'm so tired.

1035
00:51:42,138 --> 00:51:43,939
You guys did a great job.

1036
00:51:44,140 --> 00:51:45,574
- Isn't it freezing?
- Isn't it cold?

1037
00:51:48,378 --> 00:51:49,712
You know,

1038
00:51:49,713 --> 00:51:52,047
they're a debating club.

1039
00:51:53,149 --> 00:51:54,850
The other team relied
way too much on emotion.

1040
00:51:54,851 --> 00:51:57,619
I got the sense that they preferred...

1041
00:51:57,620 --> 00:51:58,920
a more logical approach.

1042
00:51:58,921 --> 00:52:01,890
They were all so confident expressing
their opinions and didn't hold back,

1043
00:52:01,891 --> 00:52:03,892
so I thought they might be
in some kind of debate club.

1044
00:52:03,893 --> 00:52:05,627
(This college debate club is
more logic-driven.)

1045
00:52:05,729 --> 00:52:09,631
They'd already made up their minds.

1046
00:52:12,001 --> 00:52:13,001
Is it out already?

1047
00:52:13,937 --> 00:52:17,439
We will now reveal
the jury's voting results.

1048
00:52:17,774 --> 00:52:19,607
Round One.

1049
00:52:20,543 --> 00:52:23,144
Not guilty, 15 votes.

1050
00:52:23,613 --> 00:52:26,348
Guilty, 18 votes.

1051
00:52:29,352 --> 00:52:34,122
With 18 out of 33 votes,

1052
00:52:34,291 --> 00:52:37,526
the Guilty Team takes the win.

1053
00:52:38,461 --> 00:52:40,529
(The Innocent Team loses.)

1054
00:52:40,630 --> 00:52:41,730
That's pretty close.

1055
00:52:42,799 --> 00:52:44,299
More of them voted guilty.

1056
00:52:45,602 --> 00:52:47,937
We won this round, but...

1057
00:52:49,872 --> 00:52:51,573
If only we could've convinced
two more people.

1058
00:52:52,342 --> 00:52:53,275
I know.

1059
00:52:53,576 --> 00:52:55,877
It's okay. We did well.

1060
00:52:57,180 --> 00:52:58,247
I felt really conflicted.

1061
00:52:58,848 --> 00:53:00,549
It wasn't about losing the prize money.

1062
00:53:01,184 --> 00:53:05,087
The fact that we had to choose someone
from our own group to be eliminated...

1063
00:53:05,088 --> 00:53:06,722
just felt really cruel.

1064
00:53:07,790 --> 00:53:09,491
Joining a debate club has...

1065
00:53:09,492 --> 00:53:11,893
never even crossed my mind.

1066
00:53:11,894 --> 00:53:14,628
I don't understand
why you'd want to spend your free time...

1067
00:53:14,629 --> 00:53:16,197
in a debate club.

1068
00:53:16,499 --> 00:53:19,701
They don't really like emotional appeals.

1069
00:53:20,136 --> 00:53:20,936
They're all thinkers.

1070
00:53:21,738 --> 00:53:23,238
Thinkers' club.

1071
00:53:23,373 --> 00:53:24,740
Debate thinkers.

1072
00:53:25,008 --> 00:53:27,409
They were such deep thinkers
that they absolutely loved it...

1073
00:53:27,710 --> 00:53:30,345
whenever I broke things down for them.

1074
00:53:31,281 --> 00:53:33,549
- This is the sad reality.
- I know.

1075
00:53:34,751 --> 00:53:35,684
Let's go.

1076
00:53:36,319 --> 00:53:37,252
This is sad.

1077
00:53:37,954 --> 00:53:39,888
We should've won this battle.

1078
00:53:40,390 --> 00:53:41,256
I know.

1079
00:53:41,758 --> 00:53:43,759
This topic was ridiculous.

1080
00:53:44,494 --> 00:53:45,894
We really had an advantage.

1081
00:53:47,830 --> 00:53:49,264
You never know what people think.

1082
00:53:53,870 --> 00:53:56,405
Round Two will now begin.

1083
00:53:58,581 --> 00:53:59,274
What's this?

1084
00:53:59,275 --> 00:54:00,609
(Round Two case)

1085
00:54:01,511 --> 00:54:04,279
"Lawyer B is defending Person C,
who murdered a boy."

1086
00:54:05,214 --> 00:54:07,849
Lawyer B is defending Person C,

1087
00:54:07,850 --> 00:54:09,784
who murdered a boy.

1088
00:54:10,653 --> 00:54:13,154
In addition to killing the boy,
Person C confessed...

1089
00:54:13,623 --> 00:54:17,292
that they also killed a girl
and abandoned her body.

1090
00:54:18,728 --> 00:54:21,997
The girl's parents believe
that she might be alive...

1091
00:54:22,298 --> 00:54:24,332
and are searching everywhere for her.

1092
00:54:25,344 --> 00:54:27,780
They went to Lawyer B...

1093
00:54:27,805 --> 00:54:29,404
and asked if they knew
what happened to her.

1094
00:54:29,906 --> 00:54:32,107
Lawyer B said they didn't know.

1095
00:54:33,743 --> 00:54:35,343
Lawyer B then defended Person C in court,

1096
00:54:35,344 --> 00:54:39,047
trying to get their sentence reduced
for the boy's murder.

1097
00:54:39,916 --> 00:54:43,552
And they never mentioned the girl
in the end.

1098
00:54:45,121 --> 00:54:49,324
Is Lawyer B innocent or guilty?

1099
00:54:54,997 --> 00:54:57,398
The Guilty Team is at a disadvantage...

1100
00:54:57,533 --> 00:55:00,969
(A topic that favors the Guilty Team)

1101
00:55:00,970 --> 00:55:03,838
If you look at it purely emotionally,

1102
00:55:04,140 --> 00:55:06,842
you might think, "How could
someone do something like this?"

1103
00:55:06,843 --> 00:55:08,477
So you might immediately think
the person is guilty.

1104
00:55:08,611 --> 00:55:10,111
But I think...

1105
00:55:10,112 --> 00:55:12,080
this was the same logic
the Defense Team used...

1106
00:55:12,081 --> 00:55:13,281
in Round One.

1107
00:55:13,282 --> 00:55:16,017
It's an emotional, intuitive way
of looking at it.

1108
00:55:16,018 --> 00:55:17,252
"Shouldn't they have done this?"

1109
00:55:17,253 --> 00:55:20,056
The first round turned out to be
the exact opposite of what I expected,

1110
00:55:20,057 --> 00:55:23,325
so this time, I tried to approach it
from the completely opposite angle.

1111
00:55:23,326 --> 00:55:25,661
(Predicting a victory
for the Innocent Team)

1112
00:55:25,662 --> 00:55:28,496
When I was assigning the teams,
my goal was to make the score 1 to 1.

1113
00:55:28,497 --> 00:55:31,633
I wanted each team to win one round...

1114
00:55:31,802 --> 00:55:33,869
so they'd each have a shot
at winning the money.

1115
00:55:34,270 --> 00:55:37,639
I couldn't change the team assignments,

1116
00:55:37,640 --> 00:55:39,441
but I could decide
which side each team would argue for.

1117
00:55:39,442 --> 00:55:41,509
Michael's team is...

1118
00:55:41,978 --> 00:55:43,645
- still the Guilty Team.
- Okay.

1119
00:55:44,680 --> 00:55:46,614
Superman's team is still
the Innocent Team.

1120
00:55:46,615 --> 00:55:47,089
Let's go.

1121
00:55:47,090 --> 00:55:49,217
(The Innocent Team: Superman, Nangja,
Dark Knight, Benjamin, Sugar, Genie)

1122
00:55:49,218 --> 00:55:50,618
Let's see. What is this about?

1123
00:55:51,154 --> 00:55:53,722
- "Lawyer B..."
- This is about a lawyer.

1124
00:55:54,224 --> 00:55:56,892
From a moral and ethical standpoint,
the lawyer is clearly guilty.

1125
00:55:56,893 --> 00:55:59,495
Earlier, the jury challenged
whether it was actually ethical.

1126
00:55:59,496 --> 00:56:02,097
They asked whether we were looking
at it ethically or from another viewpoint.

1127
00:56:02,431 --> 00:56:05,100
I know what argument
the other team will make.

1128
00:56:05,835 --> 00:56:07,504
Their argument will be...

1129
00:56:07,505 --> 00:56:08,937
that as a lawyer,

1130
00:56:08,938 --> 00:56:12,107
they have a duty
to protect their client...

1131
00:56:12,108 --> 00:56:14,442
and keep confidential information private.

1132
00:56:14,443 --> 00:56:16,878
- Right.
- If that's their logic,

1133
00:56:16,879 --> 00:56:18,747
we need to build our guilty argument
around that.

1134
00:56:18,748 --> 00:56:20,649
The lawyer only agreed to defend them...

1135
00:56:20,650 --> 00:56:22,317
- for the boy's murder.
- You're right.

1136
00:56:22,318 --> 00:56:24,286
The girl's murder is a separate issue,

1137
00:56:24,287 --> 00:56:26,955
- Right.
- so the lawyer should disclose it.

1138
00:56:26,956 --> 00:56:28,890
They weren't hired to represent them
in the girl's murder case.

1139
00:56:28,891 --> 00:56:30,025
Exactly.

1140
00:56:30,259 --> 00:56:32,293
Here's how we should frame the argument.

1141
00:56:32,496 --> 00:56:35,964
Earlier, 18 people voted guilty.

1142
00:56:36,299 --> 00:56:37,266
That means...

1143
00:56:38,234 --> 00:56:39,634
they basically imposed a legal judgment...

1144
00:56:39,936 --> 00:56:42,271
that the reporter had
a professional duty...

1145
00:56:43,072 --> 00:56:44,972
to report the story.

1146
00:56:45,408 --> 00:56:46,809
We can apply the same logic here.

1147
00:56:46,810 --> 00:56:49,811
They found the reporter guilty
of neglecting their duty.

1148
00:56:50,213 --> 00:56:53,715
The lawyer was simply carrying out
their professional duty.

1149
00:56:53,716 --> 00:56:56,752
If you now vote guilty,
you're contradicting your own logic.

1150
00:56:57,553 --> 00:56:58,586
I like that.

1151
00:56:58,587 --> 00:57:00,088
That's a good approach.

1152
00:57:00,089 --> 00:57:02,957
This is a good angle for us.

1153
00:57:03,125 --> 00:57:05,693
Exposing the contradiction
in their own reasoning...

1154
00:57:05,694 --> 00:57:07,161
should be our first priority.

1155
00:57:08,231 --> 00:57:09,665
I have a child,

1156
00:57:09,666 --> 00:57:12,034
so I can also speak to this
from a father's perspective.

1157
00:57:12,035 --> 00:57:12,935
That sounds good.

1158
00:57:13,102 --> 00:57:14,936
Should you and Ted do this, then?

1159
00:57:14,937 --> 00:57:16,204
That'd be best.

1160
00:57:16,205 --> 00:57:19,107
We shouldn't appeal to emotion here.

1161
00:57:19,609 --> 00:57:21,878
We need to make the ethical case
rather than try...

1162
00:57:21,879 --> 00:57:23,845
- to win them over emotionally.
- Right, on the ethics.

1163
00:57:23,846 --> 00:57:25,046
(The lawyer is innocent.)

1164
00:57:27,016 --> 00:57:30,185
The second round will now begin.

1165
00:57:30,920 --> 00:57:35,390
All residents, please gather at the plaza.

1166
00:57:38,094 --> 00:57:40,262
The Community: Tensions Under the Roof.

1167
00:57:40,263 --> 00:57:42,431
Round Two of the Citizen Jury Trial,

1168
00:57:42,432 --> 00:57:43,565
Case B.

1169
00:57:43,666 --> 00:57:47,335
"Lawyer B is defending Person C,
who murdered a boy."

1170
00:57:47,537 --> 00:57:49,471
"In addition to killing the boy,
Person C confessed..."

1171
00:57:49,472 --> 00:57:52,474
"that they also killed a girl
and abandoned her body."

1172
00:57:52,609 --> 00:57:54,876
"The girl's parents went to Lawyer B..."

1173
00:57:54,877 --> 00:57:56,112
"and asked if they knew
what happened to her."

1174
00:57:56,113 --> 00:57:57,979
"Lawyer B said they didn't know."

1175
00:57:58,447 --> 00:58:01,350
"Lawyer B then defended Person C in court,
trying to get their sentence reduced..."

1176
00:58:01,351 --> 00:58:03,017
"for the boy's murder."

1177
00:58:03,186 --> 00:58:05,620
"And they never mentioned the girl
in the end."

1178
00:58:05,621 --> 00:58:09,157
"Is Lawyer B innocent or guilty?"

1179
00:58:09,892 --> 00:58:12,894
The people on my right
will argue for Guilty,

1180
00:58:13,362 --> 00:58:16,431
and the people on my left
will argue for Not Guilty.

1181
00:58:16,432 --> 00:58:17,699
(The Guilty Team, the Innocent Team)

1182
00:58:17,934 --> 00:58:19,768
(Round Two of the Citizen Jury Trial,
Opening Statements)

1183
00:58:19,769 --> 00:58:22,537
As you can see, we're the Guilty Team.

1184
00:58:22,538 --> 00:58:25,140
We think this case is very clear.

1185
00:58:25,441 --> 00:58:28,009
They've given you a case
that directly challenges...

1186
00:58:28,010 --> 00:58:29,677
what we normally understand...

1187
00:58:29,678 --> 00:58:32,147
as a lawyer's professional ethics
and duties.

1188
00:58:32,148 --> 00:58:34,916
They're asking you to imagine...

1189
00:58:34,917 --> 00:58:36,651
what your ideal world would look like.

1190
00:58:36,819 --> 00:58:38,587
If we want to build a better world,

1191
00:58:38,588 --> 00:58:40,088
what should we question
about the world now?

1192
00:58:40,089 --> 00:58:42,591
Is what we consider
a lawyer's professional duty...

1193
00:58:42,592 --> 00:58:45,927
actually morally acceptable?

1194
00:58:46,296 --> 00:58:47,762
That's the question it's asking.

1195
00:58:48,131 --> 00:58:48,963
This lawyer was...

1196
00:58:49,433 --> 00:58:52,901
defending the client
in a boy's murder case.

1197
00:58:53,369 --> 00:58:56,204
If the client is facing a 50-year sentence
when they should receive 30,

1198
00:58:56,205 --> 00:58:57,438
they should fight for a reduced sentence.

1199
00:58:57,439 --> 00:58:59,273
But they shouldn't be lying...

1200
00:58:59,575 --> 00:59:00,575
about the girl's murder case.

1201
00:59:00,576 --> 00:59:01,843
That's not a case
they were retained to defend.

1202
00:59:01,844 --> 00:59:03,372
They have no duty
to protect the client in that regard.

1203
00:59:03,879 --> 00:59:05,913
They can't lie about it
to reduce their client's sentence.

1204
00:59:06,182 --> 00:59:08,550
That's the starting point
for our argument.

1205
00:59:08,818 --> 00:59:10,018
They're guilty.

1206
00:59:12,688 --> 00:59:13,988
I'll make the case for our side.

1207
00:59:14,624 --> 00:59:16,258
Even in this hypothetical world,

1208
00:59:16,259 --> 00:59:18,727
the role of a lawyer is still necessary.

1209
00:59:18,728 --> 00:59:21,730
That must be
because the rules of this world...

1210
00:59:21,998 --> 00:59:23,231
give defendants the right...

1211
00:59:23,232 --> 00:59:25,733
to be represented by a lawyer.

1212
00:59:25,901 --> 00:59:29,303
What are the professional ethics
of a lawyer here?

1213
00:59:29,304 --> 00:59:30,304
(The lawyer is innocent.)

1214
00:59:30,305 --> 00:59:33,941
Their job is to represent the defendant.

1215
00:59:33,942 --> 00:59:35,944
They're supposed to present the facts...

1216
00:59:35,945 --> 00:59:38,513
that help their client
and not reveal information...

1217
00:59:38,514 --> 00:59:39,680
that would hurt them.

1218
00:59:39,849 --> 00:59:44,352
If lawyers had to reveal
everything they learned...

1219
00:59:44,353 --> 00:59:46,521
while representing a client,

1220
00:59:46,522 --> 00:59:49,291
then the role of a lawyer
wouldn't be viable anymore.

1221
00:59:49,659 --> 00:59:51,526
A lawyer who goes around telling
third parties...

1222
00:59:51,527 --> 00:59:52,729
what they learned from their client...

1223
00:59:52,730 --> 00:59:54,396
might as well be a prosecutor.

1224
00:59:54,697 --> 00:59:58,800
This lawyer only did what they were
professionally obligated to do.

1225
00:59:58,901 --> 01:00:00,835
They're unquestionably innocent.

1226
01:00:01,504 --> 01:00:04,406
Suppose the lawyer stayed silent
about the second murder.

1227
01:00:04,740 --> 01:00:06,541
Later, the truth comes out,

1228
01:00:06,776 --> 01:00:08,143
and it becomes publicly known...

1229
01:00:08,144 --> 01:00:11,447
that the lawyer had known
about it all along.

1230
01:00:11,448 --> 01:00:14,350
Wouldn't that damage their reputation?

1231
01:00:14,351 --> 01:00:16,385
Their fees would double...

1232
01:00:16,652 --> 01:00:19,087
because people would see them
as competent.

1233
01:00:19,088 --> 01:00:22,124
No one can ethically condemn a lawyer...

1234
01:00:22,125 --> 01:00:24,958
simply for refusing to disclose
a crime they learned about...

1235
01:00:24,959 --> 01:00:26,794
in the course of representing
their client.

1236
01:00:26,795 --> 01:00:28,296
Do we need lawyers...

1237
01:00:28,497 --> 01:00:31,399
who can command higher fees
because they're effective...

1238
01:00:31,634 --> 01:00:34,569
at defending their clients,
or do we need "good" lawyers?

1239
01:00:34,570 --> 01:00:36,371
What does it mean to be a "good" lawyer?

1240
01:00:36,873 --> 01:00:40,308
Someone who does a good job
of defending me?

1241
01:00:40,643 --> 01:00:43,511
Or someone who suddenly gets
a sense of righteousness...

1242
01:00:43,512 --> 01:00:46,514
and starts disclosing my crimes
to third parties...

1243
01:00:47,016 --> 01:00:50,685
after I trusted them with my entire life?

1244
01:00:51,287 --> 01:00:53,588
I think it's very clearly the former.

1245
01:00:53,589 --> 01:00:55,625
That brings the free debate to a close.

1246
01:00:55,626 --> 01:00:57,759
The remaining procedure is jury voting,

1247
01:00:57,760 --> 01:00:59,995
which will continue for one hour,

1248
01:01:00,163 --> 01:01:03,031
just as it did in Round One.

1249
01:01:03,299 --> 01:01:05,767
I'd like to remind everyone
that, in this round,

1250
01:01:05,768 --> 01:01:08,870
even the speakers are allowed
to contact the jurors.

1251
01:01:08,871 --> 01:01:12,474
This was a rule that we agreed
upon in advance.

1252
01:01:12,608 --> 01:01:16,411
The jury voting will now begin
and continue for one hour.

1253
01:01:17,146 --> 01:01:20,181
We're supposed to judge
if the lawyer is in the wrong.

1254
01:01:20,182 --> 01:01:21,950
I don't think the lawyer is guilty.

1255
01:01:22,918 --> 01:01:25,620
I felt like we were debating issues...

1256
01:01:25,621 --> 01:01:27,321
that had already been settled.

1257
01:01:27,322 --> 01:01:28,823
Both the first case and the second case.

1258
01:01:28,824 --> 01:01:31,125
We don't usually ask whether something is
ethical or unethical...

1259
01:01:31,126 --> 01:01:33,194
in those kinds of situations.

1260
01:01:33,596 --> 01:01:35,630
Because we always try to apply...

1261
01:01:35,631 --> 01:01:36,998
the standards set by the law.

1262
01:01:36,999 --> 01:01:39,400
From that perspective,
I didn't see a problem with either case.

1263
01:01:39,401 --> 01:01:41,336
The lawyer was following
the professional rules.

1264
01:01:41,337 --> 01:01:43,304
That's what a lawyer has to do
if they want to make a living.

1265
01:01:43,305 --> 01:01:45,773
It felt like we were revisiting issues...

1266
01:01:45,909 --> 01:01:48,009
that our legal system has already
wrestled with...

1267
01:01:48,010 --> 01:01:49,744
and reached conclusions on.

1268
01:01:49,945 --> 01:01:51,078
That's how I felt.

1269
01:01:52,181 --> 01:01:53,047
Hello.

1270
01:01:54,016 --> 01:01:56,217
- Does anyone have a question?
- Hello.

1271
01:01:56,218 --> 01:01:57,352
What's the basis...

1272
01:01:57,353 --> 01:01:58,619
- Are you on the Innocent Team?
- Yes.

1273
01:01:58,620 --> 01:02:00,388
for the Not Guilty argument?

1274
01:02:00,389 --> 01:02:02,557
The basis? Can we talk in the back?

1275
01:02:02,558 --> 01:02:03,424
Okay.

1276
01:02:04,693 --> 01:02:05,726
Guilty!

1277
01:02:06,796 --> 01:02:09,431
If you want to vote guilty, come here.

1278
01:02:10,199 --> 01:02:11,733
Wait. Let me make sure we're clear.

1279
01:02:12,368 --> 01:02:13,368
I'll pretend to think for a second.

1280
01:02:14,003 --> 01:02:15,670
Why don't you go over there?

1281
01:02:17,540 --> 01:02:19,374
I don't want to intrude on his group.

1282
01:02:20,342 --> 01:02:23,244
I'm pretending
I can't speak Korean very well.

1283
01:02:24,246 --> 01:02:26,714
Benjamin told me...

1284
01:02:26,715 --> 01:02:28,783
to keep the 12 residents...

1285
01:02:28,784 --> 01:02:31,519
from going over to him.

1286
01:02:32,221 --> 01:02:33,788
We're clear, right?
I should keep them away from us.

1287
01:02:34,757 --> 01:02:35,990
Here's our case for Not Guilty.

1288
01:02:36,292 --> 01:02:38,393
This is about ACP,
which is a major issue right now.

1289
01:02:38,394 --> 01:02:40,028
It means attorney-client privilege.

1290
01:02:40,863 --> 01:02:43,832
It's how much a lawyer must protect
their client's confidentiality.

1291
01:02:43,833 --> 01:02:47,135
A lot of law firms were searched
and investigated,

1292
01:02:47,136 --> 01:02:48,636
so it's become a major issue lately.

1293
01:02:48,637 --> 01:02:52,207
We need to ask why this system exists
in the first place.

1294
01:02:52,208 --> 01:02:53,441
Why is it important...

1295
01:02:53,442 --> 01:02:55,276
to the way the legal system,

1296
01:02:55,277 --> 01:02:57,278
and society as a whole, functions?

1297
01:02:57,980 --> 01:02:59,881
The right to have legal counsel is...

1298
01:02:59,882 --> 01:03:01,482
in both the US Constitution
and the Korean Constitution.

1299
01:03:01,483 --> 01:03:02,684
It's in Article 12.

1300
01:03:02,685 --> 01:03:05,586
It's because the government kills
people unjustly.

1301
01:03:05,587 --> 01:03:06,721
(The lawyer is innocent.)

1302
01:03:06,722 --> 01:03:08,990
In any country where the legal system was
still being established,

1303
01:03:09,658 --> 01:03:12,226
powerful authorities
often abused their power...

1304
01:03:12,227 --> 01:03:13,160
against innocent individuals.

1305
01:03:13,161 --> 01:03:14,428
If there's no one...

1306
01:03:14,964 --> 01:03:16,364
who can stand up...

1307
01:03:16,365 --> 01:03:18,300
to that enormous power of the state,

1308
01:03:18,567 --> 01:03:21,169
then eventually dictators can emerge...

1309
01:03:21,170 --> 01:03:23,538
and the government can exercise
its power unjustly.

1310
01:03:23,539 --> 01:03:25,506
That's why we have a system
of legal representation.

1311
01:03:25,774 --> 01:03:29,343
"A system of legal representation
is essential."

1312
01:03:29,344 --> 01:03:31,647
We have to accept this basic premise...

1313
01:03:31,648 --> 01:03:32,880
before we can even have this discussion.

1314
01:03:32,948 --> 01:03:34,682
Why do lawyers have a duty
to keep things confidential?

1315
01:03:34,683 --> 01:03:36,651
You go to a lawyer and confess something.

1316
01:03:36,652 --> 01:03:38,019
While you hand over documents
and explain your situation,

1317
01:03:38,020 --> 01:03:40,055
they might learn other information.

1318
01:03:40,056 --> 01:03:42,723
You end up confessing
extremely sensitive information.

1319
01:03:42,931 --> 01:03:46,033
But if there were any possibility...

1320
01:03:46,128 --> 01:03:48,830
that the lawyer could later act
on what I told them,

1321
01:03:48,831 --> 01:03:51,432
people wouldn't be able to speak openly
with their lawyers.

1322
01:03:51,433 --> 01:03:53,334
Then even if you told...

1323
01:03:53,335 --> 01:03:54,836
your lawyer everything,

1324
01:03:54,837 --> 01:03:56,938
you wouldn't be completely protected.

1325
01:03:56,939 --> 01:04:00,241
That's why we created
strict confidentiality rules...

1326
01:04:00,242 --> 01:04:02,376
to prevent
this kind of structural problem.

1327
01:04:02,811 --> 01:04:04,912
The lawyer was representing the client
in the boy's murder case,

1328
01:04:05,214 --> 01:04:06,648
and then they suddenly learned
about the girl.

1329
01:04:06,649 --> 01:04:08,383
When the girl's mother asked about her,

1330
01:04:08,384 --> 01:04:10,151
the lawyer said they didn't know anything.

1331
01:04:10,152 --> 01:04:12,420
But the lawyer wasn't defending them
in the girl's murder case.

1332
01:04:12,988 --> 01:04:14,956
Shouldn't they have looked at this
from a more human perspective...

1333
01:04:14,957 --> 01:04:16,091
and told her the truth?

1334
01:04:16,092 --> 01:04:18,192
In the ideal society we want,
shouldn't the lawyer be found guilty?

1335
01:04:18,560 --> 01:04:21,195
I'd want this to be considered a crime
in the ideal society I believe in.

1336
01:04:21,931 --> 01:04:24,466
The assumption
of a hypothetical perfect world...

1337
01:04:25,133 --> 01:04:26,134
gets us nowhere.

1338
01:04:26,135 --> 01:04:27,802
In a perfect world,

1339
01:04:27,903 --> 01:04:29,170
the prosecutors...

1340
01:04:29,605 --> 01:04:31,940
would do their job properly
and uncover the fact...

1341
01:04:31,941 --> 01:04:33,474
that the girl had been killed
in the first place.

1342
01:04:33,676 --> 01:04:35,644
That doesn't always happen
in the real world,

1343
01:04:35,645 --> 01:04:38,046
and that's why we're constantly refining
the legal system.

1344
01:04:38,714 --> 01:04:40,381
What is the purpose
of having defense lawyers?

1345
01:04:40,382 --> 01:04:42,984
What is a defense lawyer supposed
to do in a criminal trial?

1346
01:04:43,119 --> 01:04:47,488
Why does the Constitution protect...

1347
01:04:47,489 --> 01:04:50,525
a person's right to have legal counsel?

1348
01:04:50,526 --> 01:04:51,926
If you really think about those questions,

1349
01:04:52,161 --> 01:04:53,748
you can start to understand
the purpose behind the system.

1350
01:04:53,762 --> 01:04:55,296
And once you understand
why the system exists,

1351
01:04:55,297 --> 01:04:58,733
you can also better understand...

1352
01:04:58,734 --> 01:05:00,501
why this lawyer had to do what they did.

1353
01:05:21,023 --> 01:05:23,324
I was convinced by what Benjamin said,

1354
01:05:23,325 --> 01:05:25,293
so I voted not guilty.

1355
01:05:25,294 --> 01:05:28,429
He explained in detail
why it's so important...

1356
01:05:28,430 --> 01:05:29,898
for a lawyer to keep
client information confidential,

1357
01:05:29,899 --> 01:05:32,166
and it made sense to me.

1358
01:05:32,167 --> 01:05:34,068
I thought Ted's argument was...

1359
01:05:34,069 --> 01:05:35,837
the most persuasive.

1360
01:05:35,838 --> 01:05:37,672
The Innocent Team kept talking...

1361
01:05:37,673 --> 01:05:40,608
about a lawyer's professional ethics.

1362
01:05:40,809 --> 01:05:44,413
But Ted pointed out the contradiction
within that idea,

1363
01:05:44,414 --> 01:05:46,147
and I agreed with him.

1364
01:05:46,682 --> 01:05:49,617
I found Benjamin the most convincing.

1365
01:05:49,618 --> 01:05:51,019
I was originally on the guilty side,

1366
01:05:51,020 --> 01:05:52,754
but after hearing him out,
I changed my mind.

1367
01:05:53,255 --> 01:05:54,956
I voted for guilty.

1368
01:05:55,324 --> 01:05:58,193
We weren't debating
what the current law says.

1369
01:05:58,194 --> 01:06:00,595
He said the premise was to think...

1370
01:06:00,596 --> 01:06:02,630
about what our ideal society
should look like.

1371
01:06:02,765 --> 01:06:04,566
That really convinced me.

1372
01:06:05,200 --> 01:06:08,136
I just voted for not guilty.

1373
01:06:08,137 --> 01:06:10,037
At first,

1374
01:06:10,038 --> 01:06:12,373
I agreed with Ted's idea...

1375
01:06:12,374 --> 01:06:14,008
that if we want to build a better society,

1376
01:06:14,009 --> 01:06:16,143
this lawyer should be punished.

1377
01:06:16,144 --> 01:06:17,745
That's what I originally thought,

1378
01:06:17,746 --> 01:06:19,814
but then what Benjamin said
resonated with me.

1379
01:06:19,815 --> 01:06:21,683
If we want to make our society better,

1380
01:06:21,684 --> 01:06:23,551
we have to start...

1381
01:06:23,552 --> 01:06:25,586
by respecting professional ethics,
even if it's small.

1382
01:06:25,587 --> 01:06:27,188
That way, we can prevent
the abuse of state power...

1383
01:06:27,189 --> 01:06:29,356
and create a safeguard
against dictatorship.

1384
01:06:29,691 --> 01:06:30,824
Please go ahead and vote.

1385
01:06:32,327 --> 01:06:34,028
- Innocent!
- I'm going to go inside now.

1386
01:06:34,730 --> 01:06:35,463
Innocent!

1387
01:06:35,637 --> 01:06:36,870
It's out of my hands now.

1388
01:06:37,832 --> 01:06:39,600
Benjamin played a big role.

1389
01:06:39,601 --> 01:06:42,336
He basically formed his own group.

1390
01:06:42,871 --> 01:06:44,538
It's good that Benjamin stayed.

1391
01:06:45,174 --> 01:06:46,741
I thought about going over
to talk to them,

1392
01:06:46,742 --> 01:06:48,843
but it felt like I'd be stepping
into his territory.

1393
01:06:48,844 --> 01:06:51,113
(Benjamin is answering
a flood of questions.)

1394
01:06:51,114 --> 01:06:53,615
If you don't step back and look at this...

1395
01:06:53,616 --> 01:06:55,583
from a systemic perspective,

1396
01:06:55,684 --> 01:06:58,252
your first instinct is just to think...

1397
01:06:58,253 --> 01:07:00,888
the lawyer is a bad person.

1398
01:07:03,358 --> 01:07:05,326
- Great job.
- I talked nonstop.

1399
01:07:05,761 --> 01:07:07,928
- I think we lost to Benjamin.
- Is that so? That's okay.

1400
01:07:07,929 --> 01:07:10,131
Leaving him behind was a brilliant move.

1401
01:07:10,132 --> 01:07:11,265
Benjamin is really good.

1402
01:07:11,266 --> 01:07:12,700
- It seems like he did pretty well.
- He knows how to rally a crowd.

1403
01:07:13,168 --> 01:07:14,101
Was he the best?

1404
01:07:14,102 --> 01:07:16,237
Everyone is complimenting him.

1405
01:07:20,843 --> 01:07:23,244
Now that I think about it,
I think it's Benjamin.

1406
01:07:23,412 --> 01:07:26,180
Seeing him today, I realized
he speaks Korean really well.

1407
01:07:26,448 --> 01:07:28,249
Everyone was impressed.

1408
01:07:28,250 --> 01:07:30,885
They said a lot of students were
swarming around him.

1409
01:07:31,088 --> 01:07:33,821
I thought that was surprising.

1410
01:07:34,021 --> 01:07:37,625
I've been keeping an eye
on Benjamin for a couple of days.

1411
01:07:37,626 --> 01:07:39,059
The person no one is suspicious of.

1412
01:07:39,261 --> 01:07:40,861
The quietest,

1413
01:07:40,996 --> 01:07:42,096
but smartest person.

1414
01:07:42,331 --> 01:07:44,832
Then, everything about him started
to seem a little off.

1415
01:07:44,833 --> 01:07:46,634
So today, I was determined...

1416
01:07:46,635 --> 01:07:49,404
to make him talk a lot today.

1417
01:07:49,405 --> 01:07:52,207
He volunteered to convince people,

1418
01:07:52,208 --> 01:07:54,008
and he made a lot of mistakes there.

1419
01:07:54,009 --> 01:07:55,243
Why is he so articulate?

1420
01:07:55,878 --> 01:07:57,345
Nothing was adding up anymore.

1421
01:07:58,013 --> 01:07:59,480
Guys, you should go now.

1422
01:08:00,282 --> 01:08:01,349
Get going now.

1423
01:08:03,519 --> 01:08:06,587
We will now reveal
the jury's voting results for Round Two.

1424
01:08:07,756 --> 01:08:11,625
Not guilty, 21 votes.

1425
01:08:13,529 --> 01:08:16,630
Guilty, 12 votes.

1426
01:08:17,399 --> 01:08:22,069
With 21 out of 33 votes,

1427
01:08:22,070 --> 01:08:24,972
the Not Guilty Team takes the win.

1428
01:08:24,973 --> 01:08:26,207
That's good.

1429
01:08:26,208 --> 01:08:28,309
The Not Guilty Team wins Round Two.

1430
01:08:28,310 --> 01:08:30,644
We will announce
the final results shortly.

1431
01:08:31,747 --> 01:08:32,847
Great work.

1432
01:08:33,482 --> 01:08:34,515
Benjamin!

1433
01:08:36,084 --> 01:08:37,651
- He's really good.
- We won.

1434
01:08:37,753 --> 01:08:39,220
- We won.
- Benjamin crushed it.

1435
01:08:39,454 --> 01:08:41,388
- I heard it.
- You're a genius.

1436
01:08:42,090 --> 01:08:44,292
Everyone out there was complimenting
you a lot.

1437
01:08:44,293 --> 01:08:45,325
He's formidable.

1438
01:08:45,495 --> 01:08:47,762
That was crazy, Benjamin.

1439
01:08:48,596 --> 01:08:51,699
The Citizen Jury Trial has ended.

1440
01:08:52,267 --> 01:08:53,767
Here are the results
of the Citizen Jury Trial.

1441
01:08:53,768 --> 01:08:54,901
(Round One)

1442
01:08:54,902 --> 01:08:58,472
With both sides securing one victory each,

1443
01:08:59,153 --> 01:09:03,677
and the leader successfully predicting
one outcome,

1444
01:09:03,812 --> 01:09:05,980
every resident...

1445
01:09:05,981 --> 01:09:09,250
will receive 2,000 dollars
in personal funds.

1446
01:09:09,251 --> 01:09:10,852
- Everyone!
- Was there a rule like that?

1447
01:09:10,853 --> 01:09:13,053
- I made you guys win money!
- Please select one resident...

1448
01:09:13,054 --> 01:09:16,458
to be evicted from each losing team,

1449
01:09:16,459 --> 01:09:18,125
- a total of two residents.
- One of us has to go.

1450
01:09:18,126 --> 01:09:20,060
Why don't we make Benjamin use
his immunity card?

1451
01:09:20,061 --> 01:09:21,896
- I've been watching him for three days.
- You have?

1452
01:09:22,464 --> 01:09:24,632
- Then you're sure of it.
- I'm sure.

1453
01:09:25,801 --> 01:09:28,002
How can we steer them
toward putting pressure on him?

1454
01:09:28,237 --> 01:09:30,772
- To use that as an excuse...
- You two have an immunity card.

1455
01:09:30,773 --> 01:09:32,474
We have to make it about immunity,
no matter what.

1456
01:09:32,475 --> 01:09:34,309
We have to pick one.

1457
01:09:34,310 --> 01:09:35,844
Let's think about a way to pressure him.

1458
01:09:36,878 --> 01:09:39,112
You know what's going on, right?

1459
01:09:39,681 --> 01:09:40,881
People are suspecting you.

1460
01:09:40,882 --> 01:09:42,883
- They figured you out.
- Of course.

1461
01:09:43,018 --> 01:09:46,387
(Evictee Selection)

1462
01:09:46,688 --> 01:09:48,591
(Each team chooses an evictee.)

1463
01:09:48,592 --> 01:09:51,158
How are we going to choose an evictee?

1464
01:09:52,527 --> 01:09:54,328
This is really tough.

1465
01:09:54,329 --> 01:09:55,195
All of this.

1466
01:09:55,563 --> 01:09:57,398
From what I think,

1467
01:09:57,501 --> 01:09:59,934
since you have an immunity card,

1468
01:10:00,269 --> 01:10:04,406
I wanted to carefully ask
how you'd feel about being an evictee.

1469
01:10:04,407 --> 01:10:06,541
I have something to say about that.

1470
01:10:06,542 --> 01:10:07,910
I know I did say...

1471
01:10:07,911 --> 01:10:11,246
that once the rules were set,
if someone needed immunity,

1472
01:10:12,247 --> 01:10:14,848
I'd use my immunity card myself.

1473
01:10:17,352 --> 01:10:19,019
Then we should decide that first.

1474
01:10:19,020 --> 01:10:23,424
I'm really sorry to put
so much pressure on you,

1475
01:10:23,659 --> 01:10:27,261
but I think we need to know first whether
you're going to use your immunity...

1476
01:10:27,262 --> 01:10:29,998
so we can move on to the next discussion.

1477
01:10:29,999 --> 01:10:32,500
We can give you enough time
to think about it.

1478
01:10:32,501 --> 01:10:33,968
How much time do we have left?

1479
01:10:33,969 --> 01:10:36,104
We have 53 minutes left
to choose an evictee.

1480
01:10:36,305 --> 01:10:38,039
Give it some thought.

1481
01:10:38,040 --> 01:10:39,374
(Evictee Selection)

1482
01:10:40,042 --> 01:10:41,376
We have to pick...

1483
01:10:42,377 --> 01:10:44,478
someone from our group to eliminate.

1484
01:10:48,850 --> 01:10:52,052
(Genie, Day One leader with immunity)

1485
01:10:52,053 --> 01:10:54,188
(Benjamin, Day Three leader with immunity)

1486
01:10:54,523 --> 01:10:56,824
Everyone is just sitting back and waiting.

1487
01:10:57,092 --> 01:10:59,760
- I understand, though.
- I hate this tension.

1488
01:11:00,395 --> 01:11:02,629
Let me bring up something
a little awkward first.

1489
01:11:02,898 --> 01:11:05,233
Since you both have an immunity card,

1490
01:11:05,700 --> 01:11:07,635
I wanted to ask if either of you...

1491
01:11:08,670 --> 01:11:09,603
would be willing to use it.

1492
01:11:12,407 --> 01:11:13,240
Well...

1493
01:11:13,942 --> 01:11:17,044
That seems like a better option...

1494
01:11:17,045 --> 01:11:18,379
than choosing someone else.

1495
01:11:19,715 --> 01:11:20,881
I agree.

1496
01:11:21,216 --> 01:11:23,150
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

1497
01:11:23,151 --> 01:11:25,886
- There's no pressure.
- I appreciate it.

1498
01:11:25,887 --> 01:11:27,555
- Thank you.
- Thank you so much.

1499
01:11:27,556 --> 01:11:28,956
(They'll choose one
with immunity to be evicted.)

1500
01:11:33,795 --> 01:11:36,463
Whatever. It's so cruel.

1501
01:11:36,464 --> 01:11:38,098
Thankfully, though,

1502
01:11:38,099 --> 01:11:40,667
on our team, Benjamin and I...

1503
01:11:40,668 --> 01:11:42,603
had an immunity card.

1504
01:11:42,604 --> 01:11:44,905
It could've been a lot worse.

1505
01:11:44,906 --> 01:11:47,541
I'd just been going along
with what everyone else wanted,

1506
01:11:47,542 --> 01:11:49,676
and I didn't really have a good excuse...

1507
01:11:49,677 --> 01:11:51,912
to suddenly say no
to giving my immunity...

1508
01:11:51,937 --> 01:11:53,337
to someone else.

1509
01:11:53,515 --> 01:11:54,648
I was testing the waters.

1510
01:11:55,617 --> 01:11:56,550
What do we do?

1511
01:11:58,453 --> 01:11:59,486
Do you want to draw lots?

1512
01:11:59,988 --> 01:12:00,888
Lots?

1513
01:12:01,123 --> 01:12:02,023
Isn't that too random?

1514
01:12:02,224 --> 01:12:05,293
I don't see any other viable option.

1515
01:12:05,727 --> 01:12:06,526
Okay.

1516
01:12:06,527 --> 01:12:08,095
- Securing our future for tomorrow.
- Yes.

1517
01:12:09,798 --> 01:12:11,599
Let's label them "O" and "X."

1518
01:12:11,600 --> 01:12:14,502
Whoever draws "O" will be today's evictee.

1519
01:12:14,503 --> 01:12:15,936
The person who draws "O."

1520
01:12:16,838 --> 01:12:18,806
Since there are only two of us,
it's a 50-50 chance.

1521
01:12:18,807 --> 01:12:20,974
I figured it was worth the gamble.

1522
01:12:20,975 --> 01:12:23,211
If I win and keep my immunity card,

1523
01:12:23,212 --> 01:12:25,979
I can keep up the act
and make it through safely.

1524
01:12:26,280 --> 01:12:28,949
I thought I could coast my way
to Day Eight without getting caught,

1525
01:12:28,950 --> 01:12:30,050
so it was worth taking the risk.

1526
01:12:33,488 --> 01:12:35,689
Which one do you want, left or right?

1527
01:12:43,265 --> 01:12:45,333
- Choose one.
- Left.

1528
01:12:45,334 --> 01:12:46,500
- This?
- Yes.

1529
01:12:50,338 --> 01:12:51,371
Since you picked that one...

1530
01:12:51,372 --> 01:12:53,573
- Did you want to go first?
- No, it's okay.

1531
01:13:00,715 --> 01:13:01,682
Oh, dear.

1532
01:13:01,683 --> 01:13:03,583
(The person who draws
"O" gets eliminated.)

1533
01:13:03,584 --> 01:13:04,618
Thank you, Benjamin.

1534
01:13:04,619 --> 01:13:05,686
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

1535
01:13:05,687 --> 01:13:07,154
- Benjamin, thank you.
- Thank you.

1536
01:13:07,322 --> 01:13:08,722
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

1537
01:13:09,258 --> 01:13:10,090
That's unfortunate.

1538
01:13:10,826 --> 01:13:11,926
Luck wasn't on my side today.

1539
01:13:12,128 --> 01:13:13,928
With that immunity card,

1540
01:13:13,929 --> 01:13:17,631
I could've just kept my head down
and flown under the radar until Day Eight.

1541
01:13:17,632 --> 01:13:18,732
But I picked the wrong one,

1542
01:13:19,034 --> 01:13:20,701
so I guess...

1543
01:13:20,702 --> 01:13:23,004
I have to live with the consequences.

1544
01:13:23,271 --> 01:13:24,805
I made the lots and mixed them up.

1545
01:13:25,173 --> 01:13:27,274
The paper was so thick...

1546
01:13:27,542 --> 01:13:29,944
that they didn't really get
mixed up properly in my hand.

1547
01:13:29,945 --> 01:13:32,880
I could tell which slip was which
by the way they were folded.

1548
01:13:32,881 --> 01:13:34,081
When he asked for the one on the left,

1549
01:13:34,082 --> 01:13:35,115
I moved my hand quickly.

1550
01:13:35,116 --> 01:13:36,683
I rigged the draw in the middle.

1551
01:13:43,391 --> 01:13:45,759
- Thankfully, two of us had immunity.
- I know.

1552
01:13:46,695 --> 01:13:48,829
I guess Go Ae Shin will be the evictee
on that team,

1553
01:13:49,397 --> 01:13:50,964
since she has immunity.

1554
01:13:51,900 --> 01:13:54,435
So, as I said earlier,

1555
01:13:54,436 --> 01:13:56,070
I don't feel right using my immunity...

1556
01:13:56,071 --> 01:13:59,607
when we haven't even agreed on the rules.

1557
01:14:01,710 --> 01:14:04,513
Why don't we vote within our group?

1558
01:14:04,514 --> 01:14:05,746
Vote?

1559
01:14:05,747 --> 01:14:07,081
On whether she uses her immunity...

1560
01:14:07,449 --> 01:14:08,749
or who gets eliminated?

1561
01:14:08,750 --> 01:14:10,752
- Who to eliminate.
- Who to eliminate?

1562
01:14:10,753 --> 01:14:12,520
Okay. Voting sounds good.

1563
01:14:12,521 --> 01:14:14,321
On what basis are we voting?

1564
01:14:14,556 --> 01:14:15,856
The most suspicious person?

1565
01:14:15,924 --> 01:14:17,591
I guess so.

1566
01:14:17,893 --> 01:14:20,729
For example, what would you do...

1567
01:14:20,730 --> 01:14:22,430
if you got four votes?

1568
01:14:23,198 --> 01:14:24,798
The way I see it...

1569
01:14:25,134 --> 01:14:26,066
You don't think you'd get votes?

1570
01:14:26,301 --> 01:14:28,002
I don't think so.

1571
01:14:28,003 --> 01:14:30,404
I think that's why you're suggesting this.

1572
01:14:30,405 --> 01:14:31,672
That's true.

1573
01:14:31,673 --> 01:14:33,474
Then you shouldn't be the one leading
the meeting.

1574
01:14:33,475 --> 01:14:35,342
You're just doing what's best
for your own survival.

1575
01:14:35,343 --> 01:14:37,011
- Exactly.
- But this is...

1576
01:14:37,012 --> 01:14:37,811
If that's your logic.

1577
01:14:37,812 --> 01:14:39,081
This is not fair.

1578
01:14:39,082 --> 01:14:40,981
If we vote based on who's suspicious,

1579
01:14:40,982 --> 01:14:42,149
you'll get zero votes.

1580
01:14:42,150 --> 01:14:44,518
You were eliminated yesterday,
and it turned out you were innocent.

1581
01:14:44,519 --> 01:14:46,354
We'd be voting...

1582
01:14:46,355 --> 01:14:48,489
among the five of us,

1583
01:14:48,490 --> 01:14:50,491
but you wouldn't even be in consideration.

1584
01:14:50,492 --> 01:14:52,026
That's not fair.

1585
01:14:52,027 --> 01:14:53,127
It's possible...

1586
01:14:53,128 --> 01:14:55,095
that someone might write my name.

1587
01:14:55,363 --> 01:14:58,432
Then your premise doesn't hold.

1588
01:14:58,433 --> 01:15:01,335
Please take this into consideration.

1589
01:15:01,336 --> 01:15:03,036
I've already been
through elimination once.

1590
01:15:03,438 --> 01:15:04,638
- So what?
- So...

1591
01:15:04,639 --> 01:15:07,374
I got eliminated last night,

1592
01:15:07,375 --> 01:15:10,444
and I was on the verge of being evicted
until the very last minute.

1593
01:15:10,445 --> 01:15:11,779
That's why there's enough trust here...

1594
01:15:11,780 --> 01:15:13,481
to believe that I'm not the spy.

1595
01:15:13,482 --> 01:15:16,316
It's only fair that I'm not even part...

1596
01:15:16,317 --> 01:15:17,818
of this vote.

1597
01:15:17,819 --> 01:15:20,020
What if the person we choose
turns out to be innocent? How will you...

1598
01:15:20,021 --> 01:15:21,255
take responsibility for that?

1599
01:15:22,057 --> 01:15:22,923
Then...

1600
01:15:26,428 --> 01:15:28,062
I heard the other team clapping.

1601
01:15:28,063 --> 01:15:29,564
Can you go find out what's going on?

1602
01:15:29,565 --> 01:15:32,600
Shall we go ask if they've come up
with a clever way?

1603
01:15:32,601 --> 01:15:34,168
Let's go ask.

1604
01:15:34,169 --> 01:15:35,102
Okay.

1605
01:15:38,573 --> 01:15:40,174
We came to ask you about something.

1606
01:15:40,175 --> 01:15:42,309
We heard you guys clap.

1607
01:15:42,711 --> 01:15:44,045
Did you figure out...

1608
01:15:44,946 --> 01:15:46,280
a creative way to decide...

1609
01:15:46,281 --> 01:15:47,381
- No.
- who to eliminate?

1610
01:15:47,382 --> 01:15:48,282
Not really.

1611
01:15:48,283 --> 01:15:49,650
We were going to choose one...

1612
01:15:49,651 --> 01:15:51,586
between Benjamin and me
since we had immunity.

1613
01:15:51,587 --> 01:15:53,453
We drew lots,

1614
01:15:53,722 --> 01:15:55,323
and Benjamin lost.

1615
01:15:55,324 --> 01:15:58,392
He was kind enough
to use his immunity card,

1616
01:15:58,393 --> 01:15:59,794
so we applauded him for it.

1617
01:15:59,795 --> 01:16:02,163
- You guys have come to a decision.
- So you guys have figured it out.

1618
01:16:03,698 --> 01:16:05,232
- Let's draw lots.
- Let's draw lots.

1619
01:16:05,233 --> 01:16:07,901
Let's get this over with.
She doesn't want to use her card.

1620
01:16:11,172 --> 01:16:13,273
My mind went blank.

1621
01:16:13,541 --> 01:16:15,609
I'd be lying if I said...

1622
01:16:15,610 --> 01:16:19,045
I didn't want to hold onto it
until I really needed it.

1623
01:16:19,046 --> 01:16:21,414
I didn't really want to use it today.

1624
01:16:21,415 --> 01:16:24,551
I thought it'd be better
to save it for a bigger crisis,

1625
01:16:24,552 --> 01:16:27,521
so I could pull it out
as a trump card when I really needed it.

1626
01:16:27,522 --> 01:16:29,222
That's why I was reluctant.

1627
01:16:30,992 --> 01:16:32,225
So, our option is...

1628
01:16:34,162 --> 01:16:37,397
It really comes down
to whether Ae Shin wants to use it...

1629
01:16:38,166 --> 01:16:40,000
or whether we should draw lots.

1630
01:16:40,001 --> 01:16:42,970
Drawing lots should be the last resort.

1631
01:16:42,971 --> 01:16:44,304
- Some people might vote for me.
- I'll do it.

1632
01:16:44,739 --> 01:16:45,672
I'll get eliminated.

1633
01:16:45,807 --> 01:16:47,341
I should surrender my immunity.

1634
01:16:49,678 --> 01:16:50,478
That'd be better.

1635
01:16:52,280 --> 01:16:53,213
I'll leave.

1636
01:16:53,715 --> 01:16:55,483
- Why?
- Let me explain.

1637
01:16:55,484 --> 01:16:56,583
What bothers me is...

1638
01:16:56,584 --> 01:16:58,218
that she's been under constant pressure...

1639
01:16:59,154 --> 01:17:01,088
because of what you said.

1640
01:17:01,089 --> 01:17:02,957
The other team chose someone
with immunity.

1641
01:17:02,958 --> 01:17:05,226
- Let me give you just the facts.
- She only volunteered...

1642
01:17:05,227 --> 01:17:07,495
because she felt like it was
the only way to keep the peace.

1643
01:17:07,496 --> 01:17:09,163
Then you can leave.

1644
01:17:09,164 --> 01:17:10,264
Yes. I'll leave.

1645
01:17:10,265 --> 01:17:11,165
You should go, then.

1646
01:17:11,166 --> 01:17:12,299
- No.
- I don't mind.

1647
01:17:12,300 --> 01:17:13,869
There must be a better way.

1648
01:17:13,870 --> 01:17:16,770
I just wanted to point out
what made me uncomfortable.

1649
01:17:16,771 --> 01:17:18,072
I don't mind getting evicted,

1650
01:17:18,373 --> 01:17:19,807
and I don't want Go Ae Shin
to use her immunity card.

1651
01:17:19,808 --> 01:17:21,675
What really bothers me is...

1652
01:17:22,077 --> 01:17:23,877
that you're not the one at risk,

1653
01:17:23,878 --> 01:17:25,278
so you can throw out
whatever idea you want...

1654
01:17:25,279 --> 01:17:27,280
without giving any thought
to how the rest of us feel.

1655
01:17:27,415 --> 01:17:29,550
You even said yourself
that you won't be voted out.

1656
01:17:29,551 --> 01:17:30,984
Right. I don't think I'd be voted out.

1657
01:17:30,985 --> 01:17:32,686
Since you know
no one is going to vote you out,

1658
01:17:33,288 --> 01:17:35,855
you don't care at all about how we feel.

1659
01:17:35,856 --> 01:17:36,957
You're thinking of who to eliminate.

1660
01:17:36,958 --> 01:17:39,194
You're only obsessed
with eliminating the spy.

1661
01:17:39,195 --> 01:17:40,828
Hold on a second.

1662
01:17:40,829 --> 01:17:42,196
We're not making any progress here.

1663
01:17:42,430 --> 01:17:43,998
- You agree with that, right?
- Right.

1664
01:17:43,999 --> 01:17:45,599
Are you going to be okay with that?

1665
01:17:45,933 --> 01:17:47,535
I'm not actually going to be eliminated.

1666
01:17:47,536 --> 01:17:48,869
The other team made the same decision.

1667
01:17:50,605 --> 01:17:51,838
So I agree.

1668
01:17:51,839 --> 01:17:53,607
If that's really your decision,
I really appreciate it.

1669
01:17:53,808 --> 01:17:55,442
Thank you so much.

1670
01:17:56,411 --> 01:17:59,113
Throughout this whole process,
I felt like we forced her...

1671
01:17:59,347 --> 01:18:01,815
to surrender her immunity
when she didn't really have to.

1672
01:18:02,117 --> 01:18:03,617
I felt really bad about that,

1673
01:18:03,618 --> 01:18:06,053
so this wasn't a decision
that everyone was truly happy with.

1674
01:18:06,054 --> 01:18:09,790
Still, I'm just grateful
that all of us get to stay.

1675
01:18:10,024 --> 01:18:11,491
Let's just be honest, everyone.

1676
01:18:11,659 --> 01:18:13,526
Go Ae Shin volunteered...

1677
01:18:13,527 --> 01:18:14,694
to give up her immunity.

1678
01:18:14,695 --> 01:18:16,396
Why did Ted have to say,

1679
01:18:16,397 --> 01:18:18,798
"Let's not pressure her about it?"

1680
01:18:19,267 --> 01:18:21,668
Come on, Ted. Choose one.

1681
01:18:21,669 --> 01:18:23,837
It's just that no one wants to leave.

1682
01:18:23,838 --> 01:18:25,739
Let's be honest with ourselves.

1683
01:18:25,740 --> 01:18:27,708
I'm tired of pretending.

1684
01:18:28,209 --> 01:18:30,944
(The Community Center)

1685
01:18:32,747 --> 01:18:34,682
I'll submit my immunity card.

1686
01:18:36,651 --> 01:18:38,118
Just eliminate me.

1687
01:18:39,521 --> 01:18:40,454
Just kill me.

1688
01:18:42,290 --> 01:18:44,124
Thank you.

1689
01:18:44,125 --> 01:18:44,859
Thank you!

1690
01:18:44,860 --> 01:18:46,193
(Two out of three potential evictees
survive.)

1691
01:18:46,194 --> 01:18:47,561
- We all survived.
- Today was tough.

1692
01:18:47,562 --> 01:18:48,429
We're all alive.

1693
01:18:48,430 --> 01:18:50,030
That was a tough decision.

1694
01:18:50,031 --> 01:18:53,066
Today, we all received 2,000 dollars
and stayed in the game!

1695
01:18:53,535 --> 01:18:54,568
Please vote for me later.

1696
01:18:55,003 --> 01:18:56,236
- Favorability votes.
- I'd appreciate it.

1697
01:18:56,237 --> 01:18:57,404
You made a hard decision.

1698
01:18:58,673 --> 01:19:00,140
I had...

1699
01:19:01,242 --> 01:19:05,045
mixed feelings when I was giving it up.

1700
01:19:05,246 --> 01:19:07,047
- I can imagine.
- I also kind of hated myself...

1701
01:19:07,048 --> 01:19:08,114
for not stepping up early.

1702
01:19:08,115 --> 01:19:09,082
No.

1703
01:19:09,083 --> 01:19:10,851
- That's normal.
- Honestly,

1704
01:19:10,852 --> 01:19:13,253
people are being eliminated,
one after another.

1705
01:19:13,254 --> 01:19:16,690
They chose to give up their immunity
to save someone else.

1706
01:19:16,691 --> 01:19:20,227
We don't know what will happen
in a few hours.

1707
01:19:20,695 --> 01:19:22,262
This isn't some extra life...

1708
01:19:22,263 --> 01:19:23,730
they can casually throw away.

1709
01:19:23,731 --> 01:19:25,332
It wasn't something
they could afford to take lightly.

1710
01:19:25,333 --> 01:19:26,566
They made an incredibly difficult choice.

1711
01:19:27,435 --> 01:19:29,036
(A few hours later)

1712
01:19:29,037 --> 01:19:30,704
We have 30 seconds left.

1713
01:19:30,839 --> 01:19:32,807
Is it fair for me
to end up leaving tomorrow...

1714
01:19:32,941 --> 01:19:35,208
because I saved someone
who lost a game today?

1715
01:19:35,209 --> 01:19:37,311
She has to decide fast.

1716
01:19:38,279 --> 01:19:39,512
Wait. This is...

1717
01:19:40,548 --> 01:19:41,948
It's your call.

1718
01:19:42,183 --> 01:19:45,252
If you're going to go, start running now.
You have to make a choice.

1719
01:19:45,820 --> 01:19:47,354
Hold on. What do I do?

1720
01:19:48,356 --> 01:19:50,357
As for collecting for the community fund,

1721
01:19:50,358 --> 01:19:52,659
we have a little extra money
in the fund today.

1722
01:19:53,027 --> 01:19:56,897
- I'm thinking about ten percent.
- That sounds good.

1723
01:19:56,898 --> 01:19:58,532
Since we didn't even lose 1,000 dollars.

1724
01:19:58,533 --> 01:19:59,934
(Ten percent collected
for the community fund)

1725
01:20:00,602 --> 01:20:04,605
Can we have 2 sets
of bread, cereal, and jam,

1726
01:20:04,606 --> 01:20:07,608
one bag of ingredients,
and four bottles of milk?

1727
01:20:07,842 --> 01:20:11,378
Yesterday, we all agreed to go in order
for the Code Deduction Game.

1728
01:20:12,280 --> 01:20:14,782
We had an agreement until Round Two.

1729
01:20:14,783 --> 01:20:16,850
Benjamin mentioned that too.

1730
01:20:16,851 --> 01:20:19,786
He said the one who did
an ideology check in Round Two...

1731
01:20:19,787 --> 01:20:21,288
was even worse.

1732
01:20:21,789 --> 01:20:24,791
Since Michael and Hippo lost
all their money,

1733
01:20:24,792 --> 01:20:27,293
one or two people must suddenly have...

1734
01:20:27,294 --> 01:20:29,396
a lot of money overnight.

1735
01:20:29,831 --> 01:20:32,800
If we ask who has a lot of money,

1736
01:20:32,801 --> 01:20:34,334
we could catch one of them.

1737
01:20:35,270 --> 01:20:36,670
That's such a good approach.

1738
01:20:39,641 --> 01:20:40,574
He's the first one...

1739
01:20:40,575 --> 01:20:42,943
to suggest who has the most money.

1740
01:20:42,944 --> 01:20:43,810
Really?

1741
01:20:43,811 --> 01:20:45,712
Is he super smart or what?

1742
01:20:45,713 --> 01:20:46,647
I already told you before.

1743
01:20:46,648 --> 01:20:47,781
Whenever I watch movies,

1744
01:20:47,782 --> 01:20:49,683
I only watch locked-room murder mysteries.

1745
01:20:49,684 --> 01:20:51,351
And he's...

1746
01:20:52,120 --> 01:20:54,722
the textbook example
of the kind of killer in those movies.

1747
01:20:56,424 --> 01:20:57,424
Super smart.

1748
01:20:57,526 --> 01:20:58,559
He doesn't stand out.

1749
01:20:59,027 --> 01:21:00,494
- Quiet.
- Quiet.

1750
01:21:00,595 --> 01:21:01,929
And you know what's even funnier?

1751
01:21:02,363 --> 01:21:03,830
His Korean is bad, and...

1752
01:21:03,931 --> 01:21:05,398
That's part of the whole act.

1753
01:21:06,367 --> 01:21:07,701
I'm sure he's karen3000.

1754
01:21:08,536 --> 01:21:09,736
Think about it.

1755
01:21:09,737 --> 01:21:12,038
Michael and Benjamin are the only ones...

1756
01:21:12,039 --> 01:21:13,306
who know what karen3000 means.

1757
01:21:15,009 --> 01:21:16,609
I can't trust anyone.

1758
01:21:17,246 --> 01:21:20,648
We can't let him know that we're onto him.

1759
01:21:20,649 --> 01:21:21,482
Right.

1760
01:21:21,649 --> 01:21:23,083
We need to make sure he doesn't find out.

1761
01:21:30,758 --> 01:21:31,891
I'm tired.

1762
01:21:33,562 --> 01:21:37,064
Nangja gave me a heads-up
that I was being suspected.

1763
01:21:37,065 --> 01:21:39,265
If we'd actually voted today...

1764
01:21:39,266 --> 01:21:41,034
to decide who to eliminate,

1765
01:21:41,035 --> 01:21:43,437
they would've definitely
called me out for lying...

1766
01:21:43,438 --> 01:21:45,705
and turned on me.

1767
01:21:47,976 --> 01:21:51,712
"Today, Benjamin and Go Ae Shin..."

1768
01:21:51,713 --> 01:21:55,983
"surrendered their immunity for us."

1769
01:21:56,951 --> 01:21:59,519
"That was really cool."

1770
01:22:01,222 --> 01:22:02,857
(Posting anonymously)

1771
01:22:02,858 --> 01:22:03,790
Post!

1772
01:22:03,791 --> 01:22:05,292
(I wonder what I would've done.)

1773
01:22:05,293 --> 01:22:06,693
(Let's all make it out alive.)

1774
01:22:06,694 --> 01:22:08,762
(Thank you so much, guys.
Let's send them a lot of hearts.)

1775
01:22:09,197 --> 01:22:10,231
(Thanks to them, I survived another day.)

1776
01:22:10,232 --> 01:22:11,165
(Thank you so much.)

1777
01:22:11,166 --> 01:22:12,199
(Benjamin and Go Ae Shin are the best.)

1778
01:22:15,103 --> 01:22:16,870
Part of me felt like it was already over,

1779
01:22:16,871 --> 01:22:18,939
but at the same time,
I thought, "It's not over till it's over."

1780
01:22:19,640 --> 01:22:20,707
It feels like the end of the line,

1781
01:22:20,943 --> 01:22:23,410
but I'm still trying to figure out...

1782
01:22:23,611 --> 01:22:24,711
what my final move should be.

1783
01:22:28,049 --> 01:22:31,351
Tonight's schedule will now begin.

1784
01:22:39,260 --> 01:22:40,894
Tonight's event is...

1785
01:22:41,896 --> 01:22:44,264
"Covert Access Mission."

1786
01:22:47,169 --> 01:22:48,286
In the Covert Access Mission,

1787
01:22:48,287 --> 01:22:52,639
you'll write posts
on three different online communities.

1788
01:22:52,640 --> 01:22:54,942
